en in patients manifesting both diseases not carrying the C9orf72 expansion mutation. J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry. 2018;89:162–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Cortés‐Vicente E, Turon‐Sans J, Gelpi E, Clarimón J, Borrego‐Écija S, Dols‐Icardo O, et al. Distinct clinical features and outcomes in motor neuron disease associated with behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia. Dement Geriatr Cogn Disord. 2018;45:220–31. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 38. Coan G, Mitchell CS. An assessment of possible neuropathology and clinical relationships in 46 sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis patient autopsies. Neurodegener Dis. 2015;15:301–12. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 39. Borrego‐Écija S, Cortés‐Vicente E, Cervera‐Carles L, Clarimón J, Gámez J, Batlle J, et al. Does ALS‐FUS without FUS mutation represent ALS‐FET? Report of three cases. Neuropathol Appl Neurobiol. 2019;45:421–6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 40. Spencer KR, Foster ZW, Rauf NA, Guilderson L, Collins D, Averill JG, et al. Neuropathological profile of long‐duration amyotrophic lateral sclerosis in military veterans. Brain Pathol. 2020. 10.1111/bpa.12876. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Burrell JR, Kiernan MC, Vucic S, Hodges JR. Motor neuron dysfunction in frontotemporal dementia. Brain. 2011;134:2582–94. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 42. Arnold SJ, Dugger BN, Beach TG. TDP‐43 deposition in prospectively followed, cognitively normal elderly individuals: Correlation with argyrophilic grains but not other concomitant pathologies. Acta Neuropathol. 2013;126:51–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 43. Soma K, Fu Y‐J, Wakabayashi K, Onodera O, Kakita A, Takahashi H. Co‐occurrence of argyrophilic grain disease in sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Neuropathol Appl Neurobiol. 2012;38:54–60. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Riku Y, Duyckaerts C, Boluda S, Plu I, Le Ber I, Millecamps S, et al. Increased prevalence of granulovacuolar degeneration in C9orf72 mutation. Acta Neuropathol. 2019;138:783–93. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Van Schoor E, Koper MJ, Ospitalieri S, Dedeene L, Tomé SO, Vandenberghe R, et al. Necrosome‐positive granulovacuolar degeneration is associated with TDP‐43 pathological lesions in the hippocampus of ALS/FTLD cases. Neuropathol Appl Neurobiol. 2020. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32949047/ . Accessed 9 Dec 2020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Su WH, Shi ZH, Liu SL, Wang XD, Liu S, Ji Y. Updated meta‐analysis of the role of APOE ε2/ε3/ε4 alleles in frontotemporal lobar degeneration. Oncotarget. 2017;8:43721–32. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Takeda T, Seilhean D, Le Ber I, Millecamps S, Sazdovitch V, Kitagawa K, et al. Amygdala TDP‐43 pathology in frontotemporal lobar degeneration and motor neuron disease. J Neuropathol Exp Neurol. 2017;76:800–12. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material TABLE S1 Antibodies used for immunohistochemistry and their pretreatments TABLE S2 Reduced model of logistic regression: The dependent (outcome) variable of the model was the presence of cognitive impairment (ALSci and ALS‐FTD were considered together). The reduced model avoids covariates with collinearity problems Click here for additional data file. (20.3KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The data that support the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.',164,'2025-10-15 10:56:22','2025-10-15 10:56:22');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5088,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10156501','Guidelines of the Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy defining the rules of qualification and criteria for admitting patients to anaesthesiology and intensive care units.','Krzych Ł, Bartkowska-Śniatkowska A, Knapik P, Zielińska M, Maciejewski D, Cettler M, Owczuk R, Kusza K.','2022','anaesthesio logy disqualify directness pelod deteriorate objected asthmaticus craniocerebral 4ts 4hs patau purines peroxisomal mucopolysaccharidoses 4ts 4hs impartial unjustified prerogatives prerogative disqualify obligated dignified discharging irreversibility organizer unjustified conciliarly catastrophes anaesthesio logy nates nunnally kleinpell pivu amending kubler siewiera durek ineffectiveness bartkowska sniatkowska byrska maciejasz cettler','PURPOSE OF THE GUIDELINES The purpose of the guidelines is to define the conditions for employing physicians specialised in the treatment of adult and paediatric patients to work in anaesthesiology and intensive care units, which act in compliance with the priorities of patient treatment benefits and are compatible with the current medical knowledge. The subject of the guidelines is the patient and his/her welfare when in need of medical assistance, and when any delay in providing it could cause a life threat, severe bodily harm or severe health impairment. RECIPIENTS OF THE GUIDELINES The guidelines are intended solely for medical specialists in anaesthesiology and intensive care as well as physicians undergoing specialist training in the field of anaesthesiology and intensive therapy and currently providing health services in anaesthesiology and intensive care units treating adults and children. INTRODUCTION The Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy appointed the Working Group of experts in the field of anaesthesiology and intensive therapy who were assigned to prepare this document. The key principle of the guidelines was based on the criteria of patient health benefits resulting from application of invasive therapeutic interventions conducted in anaesthesiology and intensive care units, starting with the highest level of patient benefits, followed by situation where benefits are less expected, and down to highly doubtful or a lack of benefits (referred to as ‘priorities’) [ 1 , 2 ]. Specification of these principles is intended to assist specialists in anaesthesiology and intensive therapy in the qualification of patients who will potentially benefit from receiving intensive therapy in the anaesthesiology and intensive care units or paediatric anaesthesiology and intensive care units in circumstances which require the provision of prompt medical assistance in cases of life-threatening conditions, serious bodily injury or severe health disorders. All such situations demand clear-cut methodological support. The present guidelines are applicable to management in anaesthesiology and intensive care units or paediatric anaesthesiology and intensive care units. Qualification for treatment in the anaesthesio logy and intensive care unit is always utilitarian yet consistent with the principle of the greatest chance of survival, recovery and return to society. The purpose of the document is to facilitate risk management in compliance with the risk management doctrine. Due to limited access to reliable, evidence-based data, the guidelines are a result of the consensus of the Working Group. Additionally, the most common clinical conditions and situations that could qualify a patient for, or disqualify a patient from, treatment in the intensive care unit have been verified by the appointed Group of experts. The members of the Board of the Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy, the chairman or delegated members of sections and branches of the Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy, and regional consultants in the field of anaesthesiology and intensive therapy were invited to express their opinions. each of the experts assessed the correctness of decisions in individual clinical scenarios on a scale of 0–100, where 0 meant “I completely disagree with admitting the patient to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit or paediatric anaesthesiology and intensive care unit”, and 100 meant “I completely agree with admitting the patient to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit or paediatric anaesthesiology and intensive care unit”. The score to each answer was averaged. The analysis was the experts’ subjective assessment, and therefore it should only play an additional, advisory role in making clinical decisions. DISCUSSION OF RECOMMENDATIONS The task of intensive therapy is the application of advanced, highly specialized therapeutic methods and techniques by highly qualified medical personnel to save lives and restore or improve the health of the most seriously ill patients. When taking qualification-related decisions, the following should be considered: directness and degree of threat to life and health (i.e., failure or dysfunction, degree of severity or progression and organ damage), reversibility of the disease process, comorbidities, and the results of accessory examinations (imaging, laboratory tests, etc.) The results of accessory examinations cannot be an independent criterion; they should be regarded as one of the ways of assessing whether the previously indicated general medical criteria have been met. The main aims of accessory examinations are to determine more conclusively the patient’s condition and to establish an appropriate prognosis. The patient’s chronological age should not be a criterion; instead, his/her biological and functional status as well as the degree of impairment, disability or frailty should be taken into account. It is not recommended to qualify patients for admission to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit solely on the basis of numerical data or prognostic scoring scales which are only of advisory nature. The recommended scales include APACHe II, SAPS II and SOFA for adults, and PIM, PRISM and PelOD for children. The activities of emergency response teams, for instance, may also be helpful in observing the trend of changes in those individuals who may require hospitalization in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. In order to establish a full clinical picture of a patient’s condition, all the obtained information should be put together and analysed as a whole. A patient with a life-threatening condition is a priority before a patient with a health-threatening condition. However, the degree of threat in each of them should also be considered. Therefore, if a patient with a life-threatening condition is not in direct danger of death and his/her condition does not require immediate interventions, while a patient with a health-threatening condition needs immediate medical assistance, the latter should unusually be admitted to the anaesthesiology and care therapy unit first. The procedures should consider the hospital environment, equipment and working conditions of the personnel, with possible rotation between the existing schemes (rotation of departments, staff, work of treatment teams, the order of admissions and procedures performed, or their temporary suspension). It should be made sure that the hospital care team members in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit are provided with medical supplies, drugs, personal protective equipment and medical equipment, in accordance with the specified organizational standards of health care that are to be applied if serious damage to a patient’s health is likely to occur in the near future. Under such circumstances, an individual in a state of health emergency should be attended to first. If life-threatening conditions of patients are comparable, rescue activities should be carried out first in the patient whose chances of survival are higher. Prioritizing and qualifying patients for treatment in anaesthesiology and intensive care units Priority Description Comments 1 The patient will benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Patients in an imminent life-threatening condition, i.e. critically ill, with a previously known and potentially reversible cause of a life-threatening condition; patients requiring continuous, invasive and advanced monitoring of vital signs, procedures and methods normally available in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit and not available in any other ward (unit) of the hospital [ 3 ]; patients requiring permanent presence of anaesthesiology and intensive therapy specialists on the ward as well as nursing staff qualified in intensive care and supervision of intensive care patients. Such admissions and employed human and technological resources are conducive to improving the patient’s prognosis and quality of life. 2 The patient is likely to benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Patients in an imminent health-threatening condition, unstable, with a diagnosed or suspected but potentially reversible cause of a life-threatening condition; patients requiring procedures and methods available in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit as standard and not available in any other wards (unit) of the hospital [ 3 ]; patients requiring continuous, often invasive and advanced monitoring of life functions; patients requiring intensive and qualified nursing care and treatment by specialists in anaesthesiology and intensive care and their continuous supervision. Such interventions are likely to improve the patients’ prognosis and quality of life. 3 It is not known whether the patient will benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Patients in an imminent health-threatening condition but stable, previously treated in other hospital wards; patients whose condition may potentially deteriorate or become life-threatening and require the use of medical procedures, methods and techniques available as standard only in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit [ 3 ]. Medical interventions undertaken in such patients may contribute to improvement of their prognosis or there may not be a positive effect improving prognosis. Such patients constitute a group in which, potentially, the policy of not initiating futile therapy will be applied [ 4 , 5 ]. 4 The patient will NOT benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Patients who are not in an imminent life-threatening condition, stable, who from a medical and organizational point of view can be treated or monitored in other hospital wards; in whom it is not necessary to implement methods and techniques available as standard in anaesthesiology and intensive care unit; patients requiring only intensive medical care understood as increased supervision and provision of intensive care, i.e. patients who are “too healthy” to benefit from hospitalization in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. This does not mean that some patients from this group will not meet the criteria for admission to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit in the future due to a change in their general condition and becoming unstable. Patients with a potentially irreversible cause of a life- or health-threatening condition, in whom implementation of methods and procedures available as standard in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit [ 3 ] will not improve their prognosis and will not stop the inevitable progression of a fatal disease and prevent death; such patients who will not benefit from intensive care should be provided with palliative care instead of initiating futile therapy in them [ 4 , 5 ], i.e. patients who are “too ill” to benefit from hospitalization in the anaesthesiology and intensive care since their negative prognosis will not change. Patients who legally objected to admission and treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit using life support equipment and methods available only in an anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Organ donors or potential organ donors are excluded from this group. Open in a new tab Selected clinical situations (defined in accordance with the current national and/or international guidelines) in favour of admitting the patient for treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit – Expert compliance > 75% Clinical scenario Severe acute pancreatitis Severe metabolic disorders Subarachnoid haemorrhage with consciousness disorders Acute respiratory failure in status asthmaticus Acute respiratory failure in exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (which has not reached end-stage failure) Acute respiratory failure of extrapulmonary aetiology Acute liver failure eligible for liver dialysis Acute metabolic complications in diabetes mellitus Postoperative patients requiring special monitoring methods or organ function support Hypertensive crisis with organ complications Status epilepticus Craniocerebral trauma with severe disorders of consciousness and/or acute respiratory failure Multiple organ trauma Anaphylactic shock Distributive shock Cardiogenic shock Haemorrhagic shock Septic shock, sepsis Pulmonary embolism with respiratory and/or circulatory failure Poisoning with drugs or other agents with severe disorders of consciousness and/or acute respiratory failure Cardiac arrest in the course of a potentially reversible cause (“4Ts, 4Hs”) Acute respiratory distress syndrome (severe) Open in a new tab Selected clinical situations (defined in accordance with the current national and/or international guidelines) where the decision to admit a patient to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit is “DEBATABLE” – Expert compliance 25–75% Clinical scenario Chromosomal aberrations: Edwards’ syndrome (trisomy 18), Patau’ syndrome (trisomy 13), Down’s syndrome (trisomy 21 – with complex defects, especially of the heart) Cancers depending on the stage and grade Genetically determined metabolic diseases, disorders of metabolism of amino acids, lipids, purines, carbohydrates, and metals; peroxisomal, lysosomal diseases; mucopolysaccharidoses Acquired syndromes with end-stage multiple organ failure in children, accompanying other congenital defects Malnutrition (cachexia) Surgical complications beyond their treatment Acute kidney injury eligible for renal replacement therapy Infectious complications without a possibility of carrying out effective causal treatment Chronic respiratory failure requiring non-invasive ventilation Diseases of the peripheral nervous system eligible for therapeutic plasma exchange Condition after at least two ineffective bone marrow transplants due to onco-haematological disease with advanced graft versus host disease Stroke requiring management available only in anaesthesiology and intensive care units Multiple trauma Injury to the central nervous system in children resulting from perinatal trauma, intrauterine hypoxia, birth asphyxia, encephalopathy Infections and their critical consequences without a possibility of effective causal treatment Exacerbation of chronic liver failure Cardiac arrest due to a cause other than that described in the rule “4Ts, 4Hs” Acute respiratory distress syndrome (mild) Acute respiratory distress syndrome (moderate) Open in a new tab Selected clinical situations (defined in accordance with the current national and/or international guidelines) that favour a decision AGAINST the patient’s admission to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit – Expert compliance < 25% Clinical scenario Postoperative patients requiring standard care and therapy appropriate for postoperative departments Patients in a vegetative state or with minimal consciousness (Cerebral Performance Category 3–4) Patients with fatal brain injury (Cerebral Performance Category 5) who are not potential organ donors Gastrointestinal bleeding, no shock Irreversible multiple organ failure Terminal neoplastic disease and/or not eligible for causal treatment End stage degenerative and demyelinating disease of the nervous system in children Advanced genetically conditioned neurodegenerative syndromes Exacerbation of chronic respiratory failure in a child with confirmed irreversible damage to the central nervous system Syndromes of complex and severe congenital defects in children in the end stage Open in a new tab If a patient expressed a legally confirmed decision to refuse treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit, it should not be questioned or undermined. each patient should be treated individually, and it should be made sure that the patient agreed or refused to undergo intensive therapy procedures (the steps should preferably be taken in advance). Before giving consent or refusing the treatment, the patient should be thoroughly informed about the consequences of his/her decision regarding life and health. Subsequently, if the patient maintains his/her decision, the decision is binding for the physician. It is important to make a record of the fact that detailed information has been provided and to obtain the patient’s statement in writing or, if that is not possible, it may be an oral statement in the presence of at least two impartial witnesses who confirm the patient’s decision in writing. The above considerations do not apply to unconscious patients. A person known to the patient may be a source of information about the patient’s previously expressed decision. How ever, only the statement of a cognizant patient himself/ herself is binding. Nevertheless, respecting the patient’s decision does not mean that the patient (and much less people from the patient’s environment) can force the physician to use intensive therapy methods and procedures that would be against currently applicable medical standards of treatment (current medical knowledge). It is unacceptable to enforce a decision on admission to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit based on grounds other than medical. Any medical specialist is allowed to refer a patient to intensive care treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit if, according to the doctor’s assessment and current medical knowledge, the patient will benefit from the treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Justification of the doctor’s decision about the patient’s hospitalization in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit is verified by an anaesthesiology and intensive care specialist. It is obligatory that a qualification form for treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit is routinely used (Annex 1A – for adults, Annex 1B – for children), and this form should be included in the patient’s medical history (in the original version or as a copy for the admitted patients) or in the medical report book (in the original version or as a copy for non-admitted individuals). It is medically justified to admit adult or paediatric patients defined as Priorities 1 and 2 to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. Patients identified as eligible for intensive therapy treatment should be promptly admitted to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. If it is impossible to admit a patient to the nearest anaesthesiology and intensive care unit, the physician referring the patient should contact the Crisis Management Centre to secure a place for the patient in another intensive care unit nearby. Then, the doctor`s obligation is to make sure that the patient’s hospitalization in that other intensive care unit is consistent with the aforementioned principles. Admitting patients classified as Priority group 4 is generally unjustified. Admitting a Priority 4 patient should always be agreed upon with the physician in charge of the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit, and such information must be included in the patient’s medical history. In medically doubtful situations (e.g. individuals from Priority group 3), it is reasonable to call a medical case conference [ 6 ] and assess the patient’s prognosis according to the current medical prerogatives; the likelihood of the patient’s condition’s being reversible should also be assessed. The assessment is based on the knowledge and experience of the conference participants. This assembly of consultants is also a good opportunity to assess potential benefits for the patient from implementation of the therapeutic methods (especially invasive) as well as human resources available in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. It is particularly important in the case of patients who are “too healthy” to benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit as well as in those patients who are “too sick” to benefit from such treatment, including those suffering from incurable neoplastic disease, end-stage chronic organ disease, patients after extensive surgery with postoperative complications and those with numerous co-morbidities that significantly affect the degree of disability and quality of life. evidence that futile therapy has been initiated should be a strong prerogative to disqualify a patient from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit. For a terminally ill patient, futile therapy means unnecessary suffering, pain, fear and loss of dignity in the face of death. Intensive therapy treatment must not be confused with intensive medical care and palliative care or hospice care. In terminal patients, the physician is not obligated to undertake and conduct futile therapy and apply emergency procedures, including resuscitation. Prolonging the dying process, combined with applying invasive procedures causing suffering for the patient, may be a violation of the patient’s dignity. Therefore, the physician has to assess whether such actions are justified, or whether they are in conflict with the subjective treatment of the patient and respect for his/her right to a dignified death. When determining the criteria for discharging an adult or paediatric patient from the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit, the same factors (priorities) should be taken into account that are assessed when determining their admission criteria. The patient should be discharged from the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit to another ward, care and treatment facility, nursing and care facility, or other therapeutic centres, or sent home immediately after their general condition has stabilized and the clinical criteria that do not qualify them for further treatment in an anaesthesiology and intensive care unit have been met (i.e. the patient is no longer in Priority group 1, 2 or 3); the patient should receive written and oral prescriptions and recommendations. Patients who will not benefit from treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit and whose intensive therapy should not be continued due to the irreversibility of the disease process (i.e. Priority 4) should be discharged from hospital and receive palliative, hospice or another type of care to let them die with dignity. The policy of planning the availability of intensive care units/stations to cover the current needs should be implemented responsibly and the methods of transferring patients between intensive therapy units should be formulated. This task rests with the health care organizer in the entity performing medical activities. From an intensive therapy management perspective, the relationship between the demand for intensive therapy and its supply is the most important aspect of this overall health situation. When the level of resources is sufficient to meet the demand, proper management of intensive therapy consists only in rationing it according to the norms of its practical rationality (i.e. not applying intensive therapy when it is ineffective or unnecessary). When the level of resources is insufficient, ordinary (i.e. when the resources are relatively insufficient) or extraordinary (i.e. when there is an overall shortage of resources), rationing is necessary. In this context, continuing treatment that will no longer be beneficial for the patient (i.e. will not improve the prognosis) is unjustified, and it removes the opportunity for prompt implementation of effective intensive therapy in patients who are in a real life-threatening or health-threatening condition. Decisions about readmission of the patient to the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit are complex and should be made by a team, conciliarly, as in the case of Priority 3 patients. The medical criteria for admitting adult or paediatric patients for treatment in the anaesthesiology and intensive care unit in the case of catastrophes, mass accidents, natural disasters, epidemics and other emergency situations which will pose a threat to human life or health on a large scale may only exceptionally diverge from the principles described in this document and under such circumstances the criteria should always be consistent with the consensus statement of the Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy, independently of this document. The statement should contain medical indications for admitting patients to the anaesthesio logy and intensive care unit and describe the algorithm of each medical procedure, thereby ensuring synchronized cooperation of the medical and non-medical personnel, including the hospital administrative personnel, when prompt, targeted actions need to be taken. First of all, in such circumstances, a transparent, fit-for-ability and fair process of qualifying for treatment, regardless of age, sex, religion, origin, etc., should be ensured. The procedure of such intensive care triage has been described previously [ 3 ]. It is impossible to produce uniform recommendations for all emergency situations, and this issue is beyond the scope of the present document. As of the date of publication of this document, the “Guidelines of the Polish Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Therapy defining the rules of qualification and criteria for admitting patients to anaesthesiology and intensive care units – February 2012”, of February 22, 2012, shall become invalid. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS Financial support and sponsorship none. Conflicts of interest none. References 1. Nates JL, Nunnally M, Kleinpell R, et al. ICU admission, discharge, and triage guidelines: a framework to enhance clinical operations, development of institutional policies, and further research. Crit Care Med 2016; 44: 1553-1602. doi: 10.1097/CCM.0000000000001856. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Frankel LR, Hsu BS, Yeh TS, et al. Voting Panel. Criteria for critical care infants and children: PIVU admission, discharge, and triage practice statement and levels of care guidance. Paediatr Crit Care Med 2019; 20: 847-887. doi: 10.1097/PCC.0000000000001963. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Regulation of the Minister of Health of 12 December 2018 amending the regulation on the organizational standard of healthcare in the field of anaesthesiology and intensive care (Journal of Laws of 2018, item 2381). [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kübler A, Siewiera J, Durek G, et al. Guidelines for dealing with the ineffectiveness of maintaining organ functions (futile therapy) in patients who are unable to deliberately make declarations of will in intensive care units. Intensive Ther Anaesthesiol 2014; 46: 229-234. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Bartkowska-Śniatkowska A, Byrska-Maciejasz E, Cettler M, et al. Guidelines regarding ineffective maintenance of organ functions (futile therapy) in paediatric intensive care units. Anaesthesiol Intensive Ther 2021; 53: 369-375. doi: 10.5114/ait.2021.111451. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Article 37 of the Act of December 5, 1996 on the professions of doctor and dentist (Journal of Laws of 2020, item 514, as amended). [ Google Scholar ]',249,'2025-10-15 10:56:31','2025-10-15 10:56:31');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5089,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1725534','Emergency medicine terminology in the United Kingdom--time to follow the trend?','Reid C, Chan L.','2001','categorising accid kmietowicz hartington okwonko cumberbatch accid 1913a 1913a 1913a rocke accid spurgeon','Abstract Objective —To determine the frequency of use of the terms "accident and emergency" and "emergency medicine" and their derivatives in original articles in the Journal of Accident and Emergency Medicine . Methods —Hand search of all articles in the Journal of Accident and Emergency Medicine from September 1995 to July 2000, categorising the first use of terminology in each original article to describe the specialty, its departments, or their staff into either accident and emergency or emergency medicine groups. Results —There is a clear trend towards increasing use of the terms emergency medicine, emergency physician and emergency department, with decreasing use of the terms accident and emergency medicine, accident and emergency department and accident and emergency doctor, although the latter group still constitutes the majority. Conclusion —The use of emergency medicine to describe the specialty in the United Kingdom is increasing, although this may reflect the Journal''s growing international standing. This trend should be taken into account in the debate over the specialty''s name in this country. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (81.2 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Jones A. Accident and emergency medicine or emergency medicine? J Accid Emerg Med. 1999 May;16(3):238–238. doi: 10.1136/emj.16.3.238-a. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Kmietowicz Z. Waiting times in British casualty departments remain too long. BMJ. 1999 Feb 6;318(7180):351–351. doi: 10.1136/bmj.318.7180.351. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Reid C., Watts B., Nicholas J., Slack S., Hartington K., Howell M., Okwonko A., Robinson N., Cumberbatch G. L., Simpson H. Accident and emergency medicine or emergency medicine. J Accid Emerg Med. 1999 May;16(3):238–238. doi: 10.1136/emj.16.3.238. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Reid C. The credibility gap . BMJ. 1998 Jun 20;316(7148):1913A–1913A. doi: 10.1136/bmj.316.7148.1913a. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Rocke L. G. Accident and emergency medicine or emergency medicine? J Accid Emerg Med. 1999 Jan;16(1):74–74. doi: 10.1136/emj.16.1.74. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Spurgeon D. Quebec faces severe pressure on casualty departments. BMJ. 1999 Feb 27;318(7183):556–556. doi: 10.1136/bmj.318.7183.556. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-15 10:56:36','2025-10-15 10:56:36');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5090,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12205522','Acquired neuropathology and its associations with key patterns of placental pathology.','Viaene AN, Steele J, Linn RL.','2025','immunostains undescribed malperfusion malperfusion stillborns abruption transpire stillborns malperfusion malperfusion stillborns stillborn softek overland stillborns liquified liquified stillborns micron unstained m0761 glostrup mab348 dwmg eccentrically plumper myelination hyperchromatic wmn lenticulostriate lsv subdural gmh cph intraparenchymal iph ivh villous villous dvm villitis fvt perivillous mpvfd stillborn maceration iph ivh cph gmh 400x 200x 200x immunostain 40x cph dwmg gmh ivh lsv lenticulostriate subdural wmn hindbrain lsv dwmg wmn dwmg wmn wmn velamentous fvt villous dvm mpvfd phlebitis funisitis 40x villitis avascular villi malperfusion villous 40x malperfusion parabasal villous 20x villous villitis dvm villous ftv malperfusion mpvfd perivillous malperfusion wmn dwmg villous villitis dvm villous dwmg ftv malperfusion mpvfd perivillous malperfusion wmn violet dwmg wmn fvt dwmg fvt wmn fvt wmn dvm dvm hindbrain wmn dwmg mpvfd dwmg fvt iph gmh cph iph fvt ivh ivh ivh ivh ivh gmh cph iph cph gmh iph ivh gmh dvm dvm ivh dvm ivh cph iph dvm gmh cph iph ivh villous dwmg wmn chorioamnionitis amnion chorion subchorionic subclassified liveborn chorioamnionitis chorioamnionitis stillborns chorioamnionitis wmn dwmg villitis deciduitis intervillositis wmn malperfusion villi liveborn fvt dwmg wmn fvt underperfusion decidual villous liveborn stillborns stillborns hindbrain harbinger excitotoxicity oligodendroglia pathophysiologies stillborns funisitis sahs cph gmh ivh dvm immunostains immunostains stillborns dwmg wmn stillborns stillborn dwmg stillborns cph hindbrain gmh ivh dvm villous cph villitis dwmg dvm villous malperfusion ftv gmh iph intraparenchymal ivh lsv lenticulostriate mpvfd perivillous malperfusion subdural wmn lucy balian rorke amarante adjemian branco zanetti weinlich bortoluci anjari counsell allsop hajnal chricton xanthoudakis bagasrawala memi radonjic zecevic cercor bhw289 gobe pontosubicular s00428 tannenberg tb12042 machin dammann leviton vargha khadem gadian mishkin disdier threlkeld stonestreet brainsci8060101 ivan minturn weese favrais looij ramanantsoa bonnin stoltenburg didinger finder boylan twomey ahearne hallberg folkerth borenstein belliveau trachtenberg dismutases premyelinated telencephalic geary grafe grafe sper heerema mckenney popek paepe houdou kuruta konomi takashima kupsky giorgadze jantzie talos lechpammer cercor bht246 excitotoxicity tb00526 khong ariel balmus brundler arpa khong nikkels pgj gordijn neuroimmunol jneuroim kozuki blencowe bahalim 1suppl maccain tuomanen bioactivities fcimb marinelli bertalot zusso skaper giusti fncel masoller crispi bennasar egana ugrinovic matute domercq mcquillen ferriero pediatrneurol mcquillen pregnolato chakkarapani isles luyt fphys ratnayake fnins redline expneurol redline redline ravishankar bagby saab zarei s41379 bykowski resch vollaard rosegger leucomalacia pl00008403 resch resch fellbaum pichler stachl riccabona leukomalacia s00381 rettenmaier reinking viaene hefti e013575 roessmann gambetti kainate jnr schreglmann vollmer shatrov scm quinlivan mendz chorioamnionitis 0b013e3181e90046 talos folkerth follett isoxazole propionic tavares gonik keech dellicour bhange tsamantioti lisonkova muraca ortqvist razaz chorioamnionitis 287e kooij handel nievelstein groenendaal jongmans 227e2 viaene nlae019 viaene santi ribbon nlad067 colford chorioamnionitis','Abstract Perinatal brain injury is a major cause of neurodevelopmental disability worldwide. Placental pathology has been implicated as a likely cause of injury to the developing central nervous system (CNS). This study aims to elucidate the associations of multiple placental pathologies and CNS injury, including more subtle brain pathologies associated with adverse neurologic outcomes. Sixty-five subjects that underwent complete post-mortem neuropathologic examination and placental examination were selected for inclusion. Gross images, autopsy reports, and histologic sections from the CNS and placenta underwent blinded reviewed by experts in perinatal neuropathology and placental pathology, respectively. Immunostains useful in highlighting CNS lesions not apparent on routine histologic sections were performed. Placental pathology was classified according to the Amsterdam criteria, and all placental and CNS abnormalities were documented. A previously undescribed association between white matter injury and fetal vascular malperfusion was seen, likely due to improved detection of injury on immunohistochemical stains. Amniotic fluid infection was associated with acute neuronal injury in the cortex and cerebellum as well as subarachnoid hemorrhage. Hippocampal injury had the strongest association with high-grade chronic inflammation, and maternal vascular malperfusion showed higher relative frequencies of acute neuronal injury in the basal ganglia, brainstem, and spinal cord. To our knowledge, this is the first study to standardize placental pathology according to the Amsterdam consensus criteria, separate out injury across multiple CNS regions with independent assessment of these regions, and to utilize immunohistochemistry to improve detection of white matter injury. Different patterns of placental pathology were associated with different types of CNS injury, indicating neuronal injury and white matter injury may be influenced by distinct placental pathologies. Elucidating the placental contributions to these acquired CNS pathologies in stillborns is crucial for understanding long-term adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes associated with perinatal brain injury. Keywords: Stillbirth, Brain, Central nervous system, Placenta, Amsterdam criteria, Perinatal, Neuropathology, Injury Introduction Perinatal central nervous system (CNS) injury is a major cause of neurodevelopmental disability worldwide, and neonatal hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy affects millions births each year globally [ 28 ]. Approximately 40% of affected infants go on to develop a long-term neurologic disorder including various neurodevelopmental disabilities and/or epilepsy, accounting for millions of years lived with disability [ 17 , 28 ]. Even infants classified as having mild hypoxic-ischemic injury are at risk for developing adverse neurologic outcomes [ 9 , 13 , 40 , 46 , 51 ]. There are many etiologies associated with perinatal CNS injury including congenital heart and lung diseases, respiratory distress syndrome, long periods of ventilation, low Apgar scores, placental abruption, preterm premature rupture of membranes, multiple gestation, and persistent apneic episodes [ 2 , 41 – 43 , 54 ]. While CNS injury can transpire in the post-natal period, damage to the developing brain may also occur in utero , as evidenced by both imaging studies showing abnormalities in brain growth and maturation present before and at the time of birth [ 31 , 34 ] and post-mortem neuropathology studies in stillborns [ 19 ]. A major cause of in utero injury to the developing brain is placental pathology. The association between these two has been assumed for some time [ 18 ]; however recent work has expanded upon the connection(s) between long-term neurologic disability and placental abnormalities [ 15 , 37 ]. One hurdle to understanding the connection between the placenta and CNS injury was a lack of uniform definitions and nomenclature related to placental pathologies [ 37 ]. In 2016, the Amsterdam classification system was published, providing definitions and standardizing diagnostic criteria for the four major patterns of placental pathology: amniotic fluid infection/acute inflammation (AFI), chronic inflammation (CI), fetal vascular malperfusion (FVM), and maternal vascular malperfusion (MVM) [ 25 ]. Additional, supplemental classifications have been introduced to define additional pathologies and provide further grading criteria [ 16 , 26 , 39 ]. Most studies linking placental pathologies and CNS injury rely on post-natal imaging studies and/or neuropsychiatric testing. A small number of studies have examined the post-mortem neuropathologic findings in stillborns with placenta pathology and have described a variety of patterns of CNS injury [ 5 – 7 , 11 , 18 , 22 ]. It is important to note that these studies were conducted prior to the publication of the Amsterdam classification system, and immunohistochemical stains were not widely employed in post-mortem CNS examination. In stillborn autopsies, immunohistochemical stains including glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP, a marker of astrocytes which can be used to highlight gliosis) and amyloid precursor protein (APP, a marker of axonal injury) were recently shown to be essential for highlighting injury not apparent on routine hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining [ 52 ]. This study therefore aims to elucidate the associations of placental pathologies, as defined by the Amsterdam classification, and in utero CNS injury, including more subtle brain pathologies which may require immunohistochemistry to detect but that are nevertheless associated with adverse neurologic outcomes. Materials and methods This retrospective study was granted exemption by the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia Institutional Review Board. Illuminate InSight software (Softek Illuminate, Inc., Overland Park, KS) was used to query an institutional database of pathology reports for post-mortem examinations of stillborns in which gross and microscopic examination of both the CNS and placenta was performed. As the definition of stillbirth varies [ 49 ], for the purposes of this study, stillbirth was defined as fetal death (no signs of life), either antepartum or intrapartum. In the state of Pennsylvania where all the post-mortem examinations included in this study were performed, fetal death is considered pregnancy loss at 16 weeks gestation or later. Inclusion criteria for the study included: 16 weeks gestational age or greater stillbirth, complete gross and microscopic examination of both the CNS and placenta, cases for which the brains were overall intact (i.e. not extensively fragmented/liquified), and all glass slides of both organs were available for review. Exclusion criteria included: fetal loss prior to 16 weeks, autopsies with research restrictions at time of consent, cases with incomplete pathology reports, cases for which the brain was fragmented/liquified, and cases missing glass slides. Clinical information was obtained from review of autopsy reports and the available electronic medical record. Gross images, pathology reports, and histologic sections from the CNS and placenta underwent blinded independent review by experts in perinatal neuropathology (A.N.V.) and placental pathology (R.L.L.), respectively. Immunohistochemical staining Immunohistochemical stains are helpful and sometimes necessary to detect white matter injury in stillborns [ 52 ]. As such, cases with available formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue blocks containing cortical white matter (including periventricular white matter) were selected for staining. Five micron unstained sections were cut from formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue blocks and stained with the following antibodies: GFAP (M0761, 1:600, Dako, Glostrup, Denmark) and APP (MAB348, 1:1000, Millipore, Burlington, MA). As astrocytes capable of producing a hypertrophic response in response to injury are not detectable in fetal brains prior to 20 weeks gestation [ 44 ], GFAP staining was not performed on subjects under 20 weeks gestational age. CNS histologic review and scoring Gross and microscopic CNS injury was assessed as described and illustrated in previous publications [ 52 , 53 ]. Sampling of the CNS was not standard across all subjects; however the following CNS regions were often available for review: cortex, cortical white matter, periventricular white matter, basal ganglia, thalamus, brainstem (mostly frequently the pons), cerebellum, and spinal cord. Scoring of acute neuronal injury (ANI) within a region was performed as follows: 0 = no/minimal ANI; 1 = mild, focal ANI; 2 = moderate, focal and/or multifocal ANI; 3 = widespread, severe ANI. Due to the age of the subjects, neuronal apoptosis was the primary form of acute injury though neurons with eosinophilic cytoplasm and loss of nuclear detail were also counted when present. Subacute/chronic gray matter injury was scored similarly to ANI. Diffuse white matter gliosis (DWMG) was independently scored on H&E and GFAP-stained slides as follows: 0 = no/minimal gliosis; 1 = mild, diffuse gliosis with scattered reactive astrocytes seen throughout the cortical white matter; 2 = moderate, diffuse gliosis with several reactive astrocytes seen throughout the cortical white matter; 3 = severe, diffuse gliosis with numerous reactive astrocytes seen throughout the cortical white matter. Care was taken to evaluate only reactive astrocytes (eccentrically-located nuclei and plumper, eosinophilic cytoplasm) and exclude myelination glia (smaller, hyperchromatic nuclei and elongated cytoplasm). White matter necrosis (WMN) was assessed independently on both H&E and APP stains and the highest score on either stain was used as the final score for each subject: 0 = no necrosis; 1 = single focus of necrosis measuring < 2 mm; 2 = single focus of necrosis measuring ≥ 2 mm and < 5 mm; 3 = focus of necrosis measuring ≥ 5 mm or multiple necrotic foci. Hydrocephalus was scored based on the description of the ventricular size in the autopsy reports as: 0 = absent; 1 = mild; 2 = moderate; 3 = severe. White matter calcifications and lenticulostriate vasculopathy (LSV, calcifications of vessel walls in the deep gray nuclei) were scored as: 0 = none; 1 = rare/focal; 2 = moderate, multifocal; 3 = widespread. Hemorrhages were scored on a scale of 0–3 based on gross descriptions and microscopic examination with 0 = no hemorrhage; 1 = small/focal hemorrhage, 2 = multifocal small hemorrhages/moderately extensive hemorrhage, 3 = severe/extensive hemorrhage. The following types of hemorrhage were assessed independently: subdural hemorrhage (SDH), subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), germinal matrix/subependymal hemorrhage (GMH), choroid plexus hemorrhage (CPH), intraparenchymal hemorrhage (IPH), and intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH). Placental histologic review and scoring Gross descriptions and microscopic sections of placenta were reviewed and abnormalities documented. Cord abnormalities were documented as being present/absent. Placental infarcts were documented as: none, one infarct, or multiple infarcts. The following variables were scored as being present/absent: accelerated villous maturation (AVM), delayed villous maturation (DVM), high-grade chronic villitis (CV), fetal vessel thrombosis (FVT), and massive perivillous fibrin deposition (MPVFD). The following pathologies were documented as being absent, low-grade, or high-grade: AFI, CI, FVM, and MVM. Documentation of pathology/scoring followed the Amsterdam classification system [ 25 ]. Grading of AFI, CI, FVM, and MVM was based on established guidelines [ 16 ]. Small for gestational age (SGA) placenta was defined as placental weight < 10th percentile for gestational age. Fetal to placental weight ratio and placental weight Z scores were calculated based on published reference standards [ 20 , 38 ] and were calculated only for fetuses > 21 weeks gestational age due to these standards. Data analysis and statistical methods The frequencies of each CNS and placental pathology were determined. To compare the rates at which different CNS pathologies were observed across the various placental pathologies, for each placental pathology, the percentage of cases in which a certain type of CNS injury was not present was subtracted from the percentage of cases in which that CNS injury was present (i.e. % of cases with CNS injury - % of cases without CNS injury). Values near zero indicate the relative frequencies of CNS injury are very similar when the placental pathology is present and absent. Larger positive values indicate the CNS finding is more frequently observed in the setting of a given placental pathology. Statistical analyses were performed using SAS software version 8.3 (SAS Institute, Cary, NC). Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. CNS and placental pathologies were compared using Chi-squared and Fisher’s Exact tests, based upon sample sizes. In some variables, when there were graded outcomes, a Cochran-Armitage test for trend was performed. Continuous data was analyzed using two-tailed t-tests. The mean and standard deviation (SD) are reported as mean ± SD. Results The database search identified sixty-five stillborn subjects meeting inclusion criteria for the study. All cases had glass slides available for review, and fifty-eight cases (89.2% of subjects) had blocks available for immunostaining. The mean gestational age of the subjects was 30.8 ± 7.4 weeks (range: 16–41 weeks; 67.7% were third trimester stillbirth and 32.3% were second trimester), and the male to female ratio was 1:1.2. The mean post-mortem interval was 73.3 ± 48.8 h, and maceration was present in the cases of fetal demise, though the brains included in the study were overall intact. Approximately 43% of subjects had SGA placentas (< 10th percentile for age), and 18.5% had placentas that were large for gestational age (> 90th percentile for gestational age). The fetal to placental weight ratio was 5.72 ± 2.68 and the fetal to placental weight ratio Z score was 0.49 ± 1.76 (median 0.43). The mean maternal age was 29.5 ± 6.3 years (range 16–41 years). Maternal past medical histories, when available, included: prior pregnancy loss (27.7%), diabetes (23.1%), hypertension/pre-eclampsia (21.5%), anemia (12.3%), asthma (9.2%), obesity (9.2%), anxiety (6.2%) and depression (4.6%). No maternal hereditary/genetic conditions were reported. CNS pathology A range of CNS pathologies were observed (Fig. 1 A- E ). Hemorrhage was present in 47.7% cases with SAH being most frequent (32.3%), followed by IPH (10.8%) and IVH (10.7%). CPH (6.2%), GMH (4.6%), and SDH (3.1%) were less frequently observed. Almost 14% of cases had more than one type of hemorrhage. Hydrocephalus/ventricular dilation out of the range expected for gestational age was noted in the gross (macroscopic) descriptions of 11.1% of cases, and of the cases with hydrocephalus, 57.1% had moderate/severe ventricular dilation. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Examples and rates of acquired neuropathologies. ( A ) Acute neuronal injury (apoptosis, marked by arrows) in the pons (H&E, 400x magnification). ( B ) Diffuse white matter gliosis and a white matter calcification (arrow) (H&E, 200x magnification). ( C ) Same case as ( B ) with gliosis better appreciated on GFAP stain (200x magnification). ( D ) A region of white matter necrosis (APP immunostain, 40x magnification). ( E ) Rates of acquired CNS pathologies grouped by injury type. ( F ) Immunohistochemical stains improve the rate of detection of white matter injury. When white matter injury was present, the percentage of subjects requiring IHC for diagnosis is shown in light blue. CPH: choroid plexus hemorrhage; DWMG: diffuse white matter gliosis; GMH: germinal matrix hemorrhage; IHC: immunohistochemistry; IVH: intraventricular hemorrhage; LSV: lenticulostriate vasculopathy; SAH: subarachnoid hemorrhage; SDH: subdural hemorrhage; WMN: white matter necrosis ANI was present in 52.3% cases. The cerebellum (40.7%), brainstem (40.0%), and spinal cord (34.5%) were most frequently affected though injury was seen in all regions examined including the cortex (29.5%), basal ganglia (21.3%), thalamus (21.4%), and hippocampus (27.3%). Moderate to severe (scores of 2 or 3) ANI was most frequently observed in the hindbrain (cerebellum: 22.2%; brainstem: 20.0%) compared to cortex (6.6%), basal ganglia (2.1%), and thalamus (2.4%). Subacute to chronic injury within the gray matter was seen much less frequently than ANI (10.8% of cases); the cortex (6.5%), brainstem (3.9%), and cerebellum (5.6%) were most often affected. Though subacute/chronic injury was not seen within the parenchyma of the basal ganglia or thalamus, LSV was present in 11.1% of cases. White matter pathology was a common finding. Moderate-to-severe DWMG was seen in 64.4% of the subjects. Half of all cases (50.0%) had WMN, and 18.3% had white matter calcifications. Immunohistochemistry facilitated the detection of white matter injury (Fig. 1 F). In cases with moderate-to-severe DWMG on GFAP, the gliosis was visible on H&E stain 46.9% of the time. For cases with WMN, only 19.4% had apparent necrosis on H&E stain whereas APP staining was needed to identify this pattern of injury in 80.6% of subjects. There were no cases in which WMN was seen on H&E and not APP staining. Placental pathology The majority of the cases examined (93.6%) had placental pathology though the pattern and severity were variable (Fig. 2 ). Cord abnormalities including hyper/hypo-coiled cord, marginal/velamentous insertion, and long cord were present in 38.5% of cases. Just under a third of cases (32.3%) had AFI with approximately half of those being high-grade (15.4% of all placentas examined). CI was seen in 46.2% of cases, and 16.9% of cases had high-grade CI. CV was present in 7.7% of placentas. FVM was a common finding, seen in 60.0% of cases. High-grade FVM was present in 44.6% of placentas examined. FVT was seen in just over one-third of cases (33.9%). MVM was present in 38.6% of cases, and almost two-thirds of MVM cases were high-grade (24.6% of all placentas examined). Villous infarction was present in 18.5% of placentas, and AVM was observed in 24.6% of cases. DVM was seen in 21.5% of cases. In over 70% of cases, either FVM or MVM was present. MPVFD was present in a single case (1.5%). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Examples and rates of placental pathologies. ( A ) Example of acute inflammation: acute umbilical phlebitis and funisitis (H&E, 40x magnification). ( B ) Example of chronic inflammation: chronic villitis with associated avascular villi (H&E, 100x magnification). ( C ) Example of fetal vascular malperfusion: two stem villous vessels with intramural fibrin deposition/thrombi (H&E, 40x magnification). ( D ) Example of maternal vascular malperfusion: parabasal remote villous infarction (H&E, 20x magnification). ( E ) Rates of placental pathologies. AFI: amniotic fluid infection; AVM: Accelerated villous maturation; CI: chronic inflammation; CV: chronic villitis; DVM: delayed villous maturation; FTV: fetal thrombotic vasculopathy; FVM: fetal vascular malperfusion; MPVFD: massive perivillous fibrin deposition; MVM: maternal vascular malperfusion Placental pathology and CNS pathology associations The type and region of CNS injury varied across the main patterns of placental pathology (Figs. 3 and 4 ). AFI was associated with ANI in cortex ( p = 0.0397). There was a significant association between increased grade of AFI with presence of ANI in the cortex ( p = 0.0043) and the cerebellum ( p = 0.0455). Additionally, higher-grade AFI was associated with more severe/extensive ANI in the cortex (Cochran–Armitage p -value = 0.0032). Though not statistically significant, cases with AFI had a higher relative frequency of ANI in the hippocampus ( p = 0.6769) and spinal cord ( p = 0.06981) as well as WMN ( p = 0.0603) and moderate/severe DWMG ( p = 0.5363), when compared to cases without AFI. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Differences in relative frequencies of neuropathologies by presence/absence of placental pathologies. Acquired neuropathologies in the gray and white matter and hemorrhages are shown. Individual values represent the relative frequency of CNS injury when a placental pathology was present minus the frequency of CNS injury in cases lacking that same placental pathology. Values are color coded to represent higher rates of neuropathology in red and lower rates in blue. A color key is shown at the bottom of the figure. Statistically significant values and those approaching significance are highlighted in bold font. Statistical significance is indicated as follows: * = p < 0.05, ** = p < 0.01, § approaching statistical significance, p < 0.0675. N indicates the number of cases in each group. AFI: amniotic fluid infection; AVM: Accelerated villous maturation; CI: chronic inflammation; CV: chronic villitis; DVM: delayed villous maturation; DWMG: diffuse white matter gliosis; FTV: fetal thrombotic vasculopathy; FVM: fetal vascular malperfusion; MPVFD: massive perivillous fibrin deposition; MVM: maternal vascular malperfusion; WMN: white matter necrosis Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Types of CNS injury associated with the four major categories of placental pathology. Shading indicates regions in which high relative frequencies (positive associations) of acute neuronal injury (red) and white matter injury (violet) were seen Both high-grade CI and CV showed higher frequencies of ANI within the hippocampus, with a positive association of 47.6% and 24.2%, respectively. The difference in frequency, however, was not statistically significant ( p = 0.1738 and p = 0.4773, respectively). A similar finding was seen for white matter injury. For subjects with high-grade CI, the difference in relative frequency for DWMG was 24.4% ( p = 0.2469), and the difference in relative frequency was 32.6% for WMN in subjects with CV ( p = 0.3540). FVM and FVT did not show strong associations with ANI in any brain region examined but were associated with moderate/severe DWMG (FVM: p = 0.0078; FVT p = 0.0419) and WMN (FVT p = 0.0538). In contrast, MVM (and both placental infarction and AVM) did not show increased frequencies of white matter injury compared to cases without these placental findings; rather, MVM was associated with higher frequencies of ANI in midline structures including the deep gray nuclei (basal ganglia p = 0.0097), the brainstem ( p = 0.1067), and the spinal cord ( p = 0.0641). The relative frequency of WMN was 36.9% in cases with DVM compared to those without, though this was not statistically significant ( p = 0.1056). ANI was not significantly associated with DVM ( p -values for cortex, basal ganglia, thalamus, hippocampus, brainstem, cerebellum and spinal cord all were > 0.05). Subjects with SGA placentas were more likely to have ANI in the hindbrain (cerebellum p = 0.0221; brainstem p = 0.0667) and spinal cord ( p = 0.1142), as the relative frequency of ANI was noted to be highly positive in these regions, compared to cases without SGA placentas. However, SGA placenta was not associated with either WMN or moderate/severe DWMG. Strong associations were not observed between cord abnormalities and any particular type of CNS injury. Associations between MPVFD and CNS pathology were not examined as only one placenta had this finding. In the three cases without placental pathology, both microscopic gray and white matter injury were seen less frequently than cases with placental pathology. ANI showed a relative frequency of -21.3% to -40.7%, while white matter injury ranged from − 50.0% to -69.1%. The decreased frequency of moderate/severe DWMG (-69.1%) was statistically significantly in the cases without placental pathology ( p = 0.0259). Placental pathology and CNS hemorrhage associations Similar to CNS microscopic injury, the types of CNS hemorrhage varied across the main patterns of placental pathology (Fig. 3 ). The difference in frequency of SAH had the strongest association with AFI and to a lesser degree, FVT (Fig. 3 ). Cases with AFI had a difference in relative frequency of SAH of 40.5% ( p = 0.0076) compared to cases without AFI. As grade of AFI increased, it was significantly associated with both the presence of and greater severity/extent of SAH ( p = 0.010 and p = 0.0076, respectively); a Cochran-Armitage test for trend approached significance with a p of 0.0765. This was also seen when only high grade AFI was assessed: there was a significant association with both the presence of and with increasing severity of SAH ( p = 0.0098 and 0.0075, respectively; Cochran Armitage trend p = 0.0048). There was a mild difference in the frequency of AFI in cases with IPH (13.2%, p = 0.1116). The presence of AFI was not significantly associated with the presence of SDH, GMH, and CPH, when compared to placentas without AFI ( p = 1.0000, 0.2420, and 0.5892, respectively). No statistically significant associations were observed between CI (including CV) and hemorrhages. IPH was less likely to be present in cases of FVM diagnosis, compared to cases without FVM (difference in relative frequency = -14.7%; p = 0.0221). In cases with FVT, SAH were seen more frequently than in cases without this placental finding though the association was not statistically significant (difference in relative frequency = 19.9%; p = 0.1631). There was a significant negative association between the presence of any MVM and any grade of IVH ( p = 0.0376). No cases of IVH occurred in patients whose placenta had MVM. When the severity of IVH was assessed in relation to the presence of MVM, there was a significant trend (Cochran-Armitage p -value = 0.0362), and the Fisher’s exact approached significance ( p = 0.0743). This relationship as not observed for high grade FVM ( p = 0.1795 for any IVH; p = 0.4898 for increased severity of IVH). There was no significant relationship between the presence of any MVM or high grade MVM and SDH, SAH, GMH, CPH or IPH. AVM was significantly associated with the presence of CPH ( p = 0.0432) and showed an association with the absence of SAH ( p = 0.0672), that became more pronounced when the trend was assessed in relation to decreasing severity of SAH (Cochran-Armitage p -value = 0.0470). AVM showed no statistically significant associations with SDH, GMH, IPH or IVH. All instances of GMH occurred in cases in which the placenta had DVM. This association was significant ( p = 0.0083). DVM was also associated with the presence and increasing severity of IVH ( p = 0.0338, for both). There was a trend noted for DVM being associated with increased severity of IVH as well (Cochran-Armitage p = 0.0210). SDH, SAH, CPH and IPH were not associated with DVM ( p = 1.0000, 1.0000, 1.0000, and 0.6378, respectively). Statistical analysis comparing the presence of SDH, SAH, GMH, CPH, IPH and IVH was not significant in relation to any villous infarction, cord abnormalities, and SGA placenta. In cases without any placental pathology, hemorrhages were observed less frequently than in cases with placental abnormalities (differences in relative frequencies were between − 3.1% and − 32.3%). SAH showed the strongest negative association (-32.3%) though this was not statistically significant ( p = 0.2958). Discussion Elucidating the placental contributions to in utero acquired CNS pathologies is crucial for understanding long-term adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes associated with perinatal brain injury. This study is the first to examine this link using the Amsterdam classification system [ 25 ] to standardize the placental pathology and immunohistochemical stains to detect white matter injury not apparent on H&E stains. Different patterns of placental pathology were associated with different types of CNS injury, including the populations of cells and locations most affected. As the severity/duration of an insult required to produce ANI and white matter injury varies across development and location within the CNS [ 14 , 21 , 24 , 30 , 32 , 33 , 45 ], it is not surprising that the development of ANI, DWMG, and WMN could be influenced by different placental pathologies. Inflammatory conditions AFI (also called acute chorioamnionitis and ascending infection) is characterized by neutrophilic inflammation of the amniotic fluid and tissues contiguous to the infected amniotic fluid [ 25 ]. AFI is assessed in both placental membrane, umbilical cord vessels, and disc sections, with focus on the amnion, chorion, and subchorionic space. AFI can be subclassified into low- and high-grade based on the severity/extent of the maternal and fetal inflammatory responses [ 39 ]. Studies in liveborn infants have shown conflicting results regarding acute chorioamnionitis and CNS injury detectable via imaging; however meta-analyses and large population studies support an association between acute chorioamnionitis and cerebral palsy [ 47 , 50 , 55 ]. Term infants with acute inflammatory lesions within the placenta have been found to have an increased risk of neonatal encephalopathy, stroke, and cerebral palsy [ 37 ]. Prior work described high rates of both gray and white matter injury in stillborns in the setting of placental acute inflammation [ 22 ]. One study found a statistically significant correlation between acute chorioamnionitis (maternal stage 2 or cases with fetal response) and neuronal injury and gliosis [ 7 ]. However, other studies have failed to find statistically significant correlations between CNS pathology and acute inflammatory lesions within the placenta [ 11 , 18 ]. In the current study, AFI showed statistically significant associations with ANI in cortex and cerebellum, and higher-grade AFI was associated with more severe/extensive ANI in the cortex. Though not statistically significant, ANI within the hippocampus and spinal cord as well as WMN and moderate/severe DWMG were also seen more frequently in the setting of AFI compared to cases without AFI. CI inflammation encompasses several chronic inflammatory lesions of the placenta including (but not limited to) CV (also referred to as villitis of unknown etiology), chronic deciduitis with plasma cells, and chronic histiocytic intervillositis. CI can be divided into low- and high-grade categories [ 16 ]. High-grade CV has a strong association with cerebral palsy and neonatal encephalopathy [ 37 ]. Some studies described a statistically significant association between CV and neuronal injury [ 11 , 22 ] while others did not find an association between CV and CNS injury [ 7 , 18 ]. White matter injury was not associated with CI in this previous work. The present study found subjects with CI and CV had higher frequencies of ANI within the hippocampus and white matter injury; however, the association did not meet statistical significance. Inflammatory lesions within the placenta (AFI and CI) may cause injury to the developing CNS, including WMN and hemorrhage, via cytokine release [ 8 ], and both maternal and the fetal inflammatory responses can potentially contribute to the presence of inflammatory mediators in the fetal brain. Animal studies suggest cytokines generated in response to maternal infection cross the placenta and enter the fetal circulation [ 36 ]. Cytokines are able to cross an intact or damaged fetal blood brain barrier and go on to stimulate and/or amplify inflammatory cascades within the brain [ 10 ]. An additional mechanism of potential CNS injury via inflammation occurs through the activation of toll-like receptors on microglia by pathogen-associated molecular patterns present on Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria [ 1 , 27 ]. This toll-like receptor activation leads to downstream release of reactive nitrogen and oxygen species, cytokines, and glutamate, causing direct injury to developing oligodendrocytes, axons, and neurons [ 29 ]. Fetal and maternal vascular malperfusion FVM (previously referred to as fetal thrombotic vasculopathy/obstruction) is an umbrella term to describe lesions which are likely to be due to obstruction in fetal blood flow and can result from a number of underlying conditions [ 25 ]. It is characterized by clusters of villi with changes indicative of reduced fetal blood flow seen in conjunction with large vessel lesions and/or umbilical cord abnormalities predisposing to venous occlusion [ 39 ]. FVM is associated with neurologic injury in both preterm and term liveborn infants [ 37 ]. A couple studies found statistically significant correlations between ANI and FVM [ 7 , 18 ] while others did not [ 11 , 22 ]. White matter lesions were not reported to be associated with FVM in any known prior studies. In contrast, the present study found statistically significant associations between both FVM and FVT and moderate/severe DWMG; WMN was also more frequently observed in cases with FVT than cases without ( p = 0.054). No associations were seen between FVM and ANI. MVM (previously called maternal vascular underperfusion) can be stratified as low- and high- grade and is characterized by both gross and microscopic changes in the placenta including low placental weight, infarcts, maternal decidual vasculopathy, and villous changes [ 25 , 39 ]. In liveborn infants, milder forms of MVM are not strongly associated with neurologic injury though severe MVM in preterm infants is associated with cerebral palsy, periventricular hemorrhagic infarction, and adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes [ 37 ]. As with other patterns of placental pathology, high rates of CNS injury have been reported in stillborns whose placentas demonstrated MVM [ 22 ]. Some studies reported CNS injury, including ANI and white matter injury, in stillborns with placental infarcts and growth restriction [ 5 , 6 , 18 ]. However statistically significant correlations between MVM and CNS injury were not detected in other studies [ 7 , 11 ]. In the present study, MVM was statistically significantly associated with ANI in the basal ganglia. Though not statistically significant, higher relative frequencies of ANI in other midline structures including the brainstem and spinal cord were also observed. There were higher rates of ANI in the presence of MVM, placental infarction, and AVM compared to cases without these features. MVM, placental infarction, and AVM did not show any difference in frequency or significant associations with white matter injury. Although SGA placenta can be associated with MVM, when assessed independently, it showed subjects with this finding were more likely to have ANI in the hindbrain and spinal cord; an association with white matter injury was not observed. ANI in these regions as well as an absence of increased rates of white matter injury were also noted in cases with MVM, supporting a relationship between MVM and SGA placentas. In prior studies that examined placentas with low weights (< 10th percentile and < 3rd percentile for gestational age), no associations were seen with neuronal injury, gliosis, and hemorrhage [ 7 , 11 ]. It is important to consider that SGA placentas may not directly cause brain injury but may be a harbinger of an underlying placental pathology that could result in damage to the developing CNS. Placental lesions which may result in ischemia (FVM and MVM) also have potentially damaging effects on the developing brain. Hypoxia-ischemia results in decreased adenosine triphosphate (ATP) production leading to failure of ATP-dependent ion pumps and subsequently increased intracellular sodium, calcium, and chloride ion concentrations followed by an influx of water. Glutamate also accumulates in synaptic terminals resulting in damaging excitotoxicity in both neurons and immature oligodendroglia [ 35 ]. Developing neurons and glia express distinct receptors across development [ 3 , 4 , 12 , 23 , 48 ] which leads to variable sensitivities/resistance to hypoxic-ischemic insults across various brain regions at different developmental time points [ 24 , 30 , 32 , 45 ]. Though both associated with ischemia, we found distinct patterns of CNS pathology to be associated with MVM and FVM, an observation also made in prior work [ 7 ]. These findings suggest that MVM and FVM may impact the developing CNS in different ways. Additionally, the clinical associations of MVM and FVM are distinct [ 25 ], further supporting unique underlying pathophysiologies. Hemorrhage Two studies of stillborns assessed correlations between placental pathologies and CNS hemorrhage on post-mortem examination [ 11 , 18 ]. White matter hemorrhage did not show statistically significant associations with any of the four main patterns of placental pathology [ 11 ]. Germinal matrix hemorrhages and/or intraventricular hemorrhages were found to be associated with funisitis, but not CI, MVM, and FVM [ 18 ]. SAHs were not assessed in any known prior work. The present study, SAH had a statistically significant association with AFI. Statistically significant associations were observed between other placental pathologies and CNS hemorrhages. CPH was associated with AVM while GMH and IVH were most associated with DVM. Methodology and limitations High rates of CNS pathology were detected across the different patterns of placental pathology, in keeping with prior reports [ 19 , 22 ]. This study expands upon prior work in that neuronal injury was assessed across multiple independent regions separately, immunostains were used for the detection of white matter injury, and the Amsterdam criteria were used to categorize placental pathologies. This the first report of a significant association between FVM and white matter lesions and adds to the growing body of evidence that both inflammation and hypoxic-ischemic insults may damage the developing CNS. Some differences between prior work and this study, particularly for white matter injury (ex. in the setting of FVM), may be due to methodology and the use of immunostains. As previously reported [ 52 ] and reflected in this study, immunostaining greatly enhances the detection of white matter injury in stillborns. The use of GFAP and APP to highlight DWMG and WMN, respectively, may contribute to associations between placental pathology and white matter injury being detected in the present study but not in prior work. It is also important to note that the few studies aimed at assessing placental pathology and CNS injury in stillborns have also varied in the gestational ages of the subjects and all previous work was done prior to the publication of the Amsterdam classification criteria which may also contribute to differences in results across studies. A limitation to the present study is that there were very few “normal” placentas within our study population. The overall high rate of placental pathologies is reflected in our cohort containing only stillborn subjects and in our institution’s patient population, many of which are high-risk patients. However, it is important to note that in this study, the mean fetal to placental weight ratio Z score was near the median, indicative of a normal distribution. This supports that while coming from a high-risk population, the subjects are representative of the greater population on which the fetal to placental weight ratio Z scores are based. In the three cases without placental pathology, there were no CNS pathology nor hemorrhage; however, given the very small number it is difficult to make robust comparisons. All types of microscopic CNS injury and hemorrhage were seen less frequently in cases without placental pathology, and there was significantly less DWMG in these cases compared to cases with placental pathology. The majority of the post-mortem examinations included in this study were sent to our institution in consultation (i.e. prenatal care and intrapartum care were performed at outside institutions). As such, the amount of available maternal medical history for each case varied considerably. Therefore, this study is not well suited to explore maternal factors which could affect pregnancy outcomes; however, we do note high rates of prior pregnancy loss (27.7%) in our cohort, in keeping with a high-risk patient population. On average, the mothers in this cohort were not of advanced maternal age (mean age 29.5 years). An additional potential limitation is the smaller sample size present when broken down for statistical analysis. In some cases, there was only a few cases that within a comparison group. This was accounted for by using Fisher’s Exact tests instead of Chi-square analyses. This may limit the ability to detect weaker associations. Due to the overlap of fetal and placental pathology, as well as the continuity between certain types of CNS pathology including hemorrhages, confounders have not been clearly delineated due to sample size. Corrections for multiple comparisons were not performed due to small sample size and independent nature of many of the variables. Ideally in a larger cohort, corrections for multiple associated comparisons could be performed and is a goal of future studies. Some placental pathologies and CNS abnormalities have been demonstrated to have strong associations with clinical outcomes. For example, FVM, particularly in severe cases, is associated with visceral ischemic disease, intrauterine growth restriction, and fetal death [ 25 ]. MVM can be seen in maternal conditions with a component of vascular disease including maternal hypertension, diabetes, preeclampsia and fetal growth restriction, and there can be an increased risk for recurrence in future pregnancies [ 25 ]. However, placental pathology does not definitively predict individual clinical outcomes, and the same is true for CNS injury. Furthermore, the precise mechanisms underlying the connections between both placental and CNS pathologies and clinical outcomes are not entirely understood. Nevertheless, documentation of all gross and microscopic pathology is essential for studying and uncovering any potential clinical significance associated with these changes. Conclusions This work expands on the few prior studies looking at in utero CNS injury across various placental pathologies. To our knowledge, this is the first of these studies to standardize placental pathology according to the Amsterdam consensus criteria [ 25 ], separate out injury across multiple CNS regions with independent assessment of these regions, and to utilize immunohistochemistry to detect white matter injury which is often not apparent on H&E stains in stillborns [ 52 ]. This is the first report of a statistically significant association between FVM and perinatal white matter injury. Increased rates of gray matter injury were not seen in the setting of FVM. AFI was significantly associated with ANI in the cortex and cerebellum as well as SAH. Though not statistically significant, ANI in the hippocampus was seen at higher frequencies in subjects whose placentas had CI, particularly high-grade, compared to those without this placental pathology. MVM was not associated with white matter injury but rather ANI in the basal ganglia, brainstem, and spinal cord, and CPH was associated with AVM. ANI in the hindbrain was associated with SGA placentas. GMH and IVH were associated with DVM. This study provides further evidence that placental pathologies likely contribute to CNS injury acquired in utero . Elucidating these placental contributions to acquired CNS pathologies is crucial for understanding long-term adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes associated with perinatal brain injury and could also help highlight clinical needs earlier than would otherwise be detected. Acknowledgements Not applicable. Abbreviations AFI Amniotic fluid infection ANI Acute neuronal injury APP amyloid precursor protein AVM Accelerated villous maturation CI Chronic inflammation CNS Central nervous system CPH Choroid plexus hemorrhage CV Chronic villitis DWMG Diffuse white matter gliosis DVM Delayed villous maturation FVM Fetal vascular malperfusion FTV Fetal thrombotic vasculopathy GFAP Glial fibrillary acidic protein GMH Germinal matrix/subependymal hemorrhage H&E Hematoxylin and eosin IPH Intraparenchymal hemorrhage IVH Intraventricular hemorrhage LSV Lenticulostriate vasculopathy MPVFD Massive perivillous fibrin deposition MVM Maternal vascular malperfusion SAH Subarachnoid hemorrhage SDH Subdural hemorrhage WMN White matter necrosis Author contributions A.N.V. designed the study, collected all data pertaining to the CNS, analyzed and interpreted all study data, created figures, and wrote the manuscript. J.S. performed all statistical analyses and incorporated statistical data within the manuscript. R.L.L. collected all data pertaining to the placenta and provided images of placental pathologies. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding Funding support for this research was provided by the Lucy Balian Rorke-Adams Endowed Chair in Neuropathology to A.N.V. Data availability The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate This study was granted exemption by the Children’s Hospital of Philadelphia Institutional Review Board Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Amarante-Mendes GP, Adjemian S, Branco LM, Zanetti LC, Weinlich R, Bortoluci KR (2018) Pattern recognition receptors and the host cell death molecular machinery. Front Immunol 16:9:2379. 10.3389/fimmu.2018.02379 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Anjari M, Counsell SJ, Srinivasan L, Allsop JM, Hajnal JV, Rutherford MA et al (2009) The association of lung disease with cerebral white matter abnormalities in preterm infants. Pediatrics 124(1):268–276. 10.1542/peds.2008-1294 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Back SA, Han BH, Luo NL, Chricton CA, Xanthoudakis S, Tam J et al (2002) Selective vulnerability of late oligodendrocyte progenitors to hypoxia-ischemia. J Neurosci 22(2):455–463. 10.1523/JNEUROSCI.22-02-00455.2002 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] 