INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5108,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12517020','Identifying and prioritising technical and non-technical skills for simulation-based curriculum in anaesthesiology: a Delphi-based needs assessment.','Vincent D, Mitali P, Eapen A, Mahmood LS, Mudigere GR.','2025','cames reprioritisation cames cames reprioritisation customised savvy gravitating halsted kerns cames isa isa confidentially cames cames weightage inappropriateness mastered reprioritise cames oxygenate laryngospasm bronchospasm hyperkalaemia transoesophageal preprioritised nayahangan cames cames transoesophageal reprioritising fibreoptic fibreoptic cames fmiec ringsted ostergaard utstein 0b013e3182207c24 pande muthiah ija ija_1254_23 kowalewski halsted peyton kneebone savoldelli burlacu lazarovici utstein bordage telder shidhani qaboos gustavsen paltved cour paltved nayahangan konge endovasc ejvs nayahangan bølling gilboe lindorff nayahangan clementsen paltved nayahangan beste nayahangan stefanidis thim nayahangan paltved e000697 bmjpo veenstra wojtowicz gcl mcgeorge flin','Abstract Objectives To identify and prioritise technical and non-technical procedures that should be integrated into a simulation-based training curriculum in anaesthesiology using the Delphi method. Study design A needs assessment using a three-round Delphi process was completed among leaders in anaesthesiologists across medical colleges in our country. The responders were mostly alumni of our institution and their colleagues. Delphi round 1 identified technical and non-technical skills after brainstorming sessions with experts in the field. Round 2 involved collecting data through questionnaires about the technical skills best suited for simulation-based training and topics of importance using Copenhagen Academy for Medical Education and Simulation-Needs Assessment Formula (CAMES NAF) score. Round 3 included final elimination and reprioritisation. The topics were narrowed down by steering research members based on data saturation in the open-ended questions (qualitative), as well as statistical data (CAMES-NAF) for the quantitative questions. Results In Delphi round 1, we identified 22 technical skills, 10 non-technical skills and 21 scenarios. Response rates in the Delphi round 2 averaged to 85.5% and responses were prioritised using CAMES NAF score. Open-ended questions reached data saturation for non-technical skills and scenarios. In round 3, we were able to narrow it down to 10 technical skills, 5 non-technical skills and 8 scenarios after elimination and reprioritisation. Conclusions We identified and prioritised 8 scenarios, 10 technical skills and 5 non-technical skills in anaesthesiology that are suitable for simulation and may be used as a guide for developing simulation-based curriculum in anaesthesiology. Keywords: Simulation, Anaesthesia, Medical education, Delphi Technique WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN ON THIS TOPIC Simulation-based education has evolved over the past decade across healthcare. Anaesthesiologists must handle emergencies with limited training opportunities. While the curriculum exists, it lacks proper structure. Direct clinical experience alone is impractical and ethically challenging. WHAT THIS STUDY ADDS Developed an assessment tool for considering feasibility for simulation of both technical and non-technical skill, impact on patient safety, frequency of skill performance and current physician competency levels. Though findings reflect consensus, they yet remain grounded in expert opinion. Identified and addressed gaps in less frequent but critical scenarios. HOW THIS STUDY MIGHT AFFECT RESEARCH, PRACTICE OR POLICY Provides framework for structured curriculum development. Enables institutions to create customised training programmes. Guides other anaesthesiology programmes in developing context-appropriate curriculum. Introduction The adoption of simulation as a teaching and training tool is rapidly gaining recognition across various healthcare disciplines, despite the early stage of research in this area. Stakeholders increasingly demand empirical evidence demonstrating the efficacy of simulation in achieving desired learning outcomes. These outcomes encompass not only the supplementation of clinical experience but also the enhancement of patient care quality and safety through simulation-based training (SBT). The advancement of simulation methodologies for the benefit of patients and healthcare practitioners depends heavily on robust research efforts. 1 The integration of SBT into the Anaesthesiology residents’ training curriculum entails identifying both technical and non-technical skills best suited for simulation-based instruction. This endeavour can be facilitated by optimising resource utilisation to enrich students'' learning experiences. Today’s tech-savvy generation of students often finds traditional teaching methods less engaging, gravitating towards active participation and experiential learning facilitated by technology and innovation, particularly within the boundaries of SBT. 2 While traditional medical training followed the Halsted apprenticeship model of ‘See one, Do one, Teach one’, this model may not be appropriate, given the risks to patients during students’ learning curves before competency attainment. 3 4 SBT offers learners the opportunity for repetitive, deliberate practice within a safe environment, mitigating the potential for harm during the learning process. Using a needs assessment survey from all medical colleges within the context of the National Medical Council (INDIA), Competency Based Medical Education guidelines in Anaesthesia, we can narrow down and target training areas that are often neglected or challenging to achieve during the residency period. 5 Using this information, we plan to develop, implement and evaluate a Simulation-Based curriculum that can be integrated into the existing Anaesthesiology residency programme. 6 To develop such a curriculum starts with a needs assessment (first step of Kerns’ six-step approach to curriculum development) which is a systematic process of identifying gaps, challenges and learning objectives to determine the educational needs of learners. 7 8 The aim of this study was to conduct a national general needs assessment using a Delphi process to identify and prioritise technical and non-technical skills for inclusion in a SBT curriculum for anaesthesiology trainees. Materials and methodology Study design It is a descriptive study where both quantitative and qualitative data were analysed using the Delphi technique. The Delphi technique allows involvement of participants from across different locations ( figure 1 ). This method has been used to develop curriculum for SBT in various specialties like cardiology, ophthalmology, vascular surgery, urology and others. 9 , 14 Figure 1. Delphi process for needs assessment. CAMES-NAF [Copenhagen Academy for Medical Education and Simulation Needs Assessment Formula]. Open in a new tab Participants All participants were practising anaesthesiologists and key opinion leaders in anaesthesiology education, affiliated with the Indian Society of Anaesthesiologists (ISA). The cohort was well-represented across various academic ranks: 30.3% were residents, 14.3% assistant professors, 9.1% associate professors and 16.9% professors. A significant proportion (40.3%) had over 10 years of clinical experience. Regarding simulation training, 56.6% had not undergone any, while 32.2% regarded it as essential for resident education. The Delphi survey was distributed electronically via Google Forms, accompanied by a participation information sheet, to medical colleges, members of the ISA, and alumni of our institution, selected for their accessibility and relevance to the research focus. The survey remained open for 2 months, with two reminder notifications sent to encourage participation. In the first round, 90 anaesthesiologists from 32 institutions participated. Subsequent rounds (second and third) were administered to all participants, regardless of their involvement in previous rounds, to ensure comprehensive feedback. Participation was voluntary, with all responses treated confidentially. Patient and public involvement No patients participated and there was no public involvement in the study. Instruments The primary instrument employed in this study was a structured questionnaire, administered over three rounds to achieve expert consensus. Round 1: Demographics: Participants provided background information, including their clinical experience and current roles. Open-ended questions: Participants elaborated on: ‘Which procedures do you believe a newly graduated specialist in anaesthesiology should be able to perform?’ ‘What challenges do you perceive in implementing simulation-based training for anaesthesia residents?’ Round 2: Quantitative assessment: Each identified procedure was rated using a 5-point Likert scale. Round 3: Review and re-rating: Participants reviewed the aggregated results from round 2 and were invited to re-rate items, considering the group’s feedback. Data collection A research group was formed to facilitate data collection and analysis. It consisted of five members, all investigators and coauthors of the study. The primary investigator was responsible for organising the data, ensuring that participant responses remained confidential from the steering group. All suggestions provided by participants were thoroughly considered and included unless consensus was not achieved. Delphi round 1: brainstorming phase The process started with a brainstorming phase where the participants (experts in the field of anaesthesiology) suggested technical procedures, scenarios and non-technical skills which an anaesthesiologist who had just completed specialist training should be able to perform. Participants were also asked to mention problems of simulation and challenges. The steering committee meticulously analysed and summarised the data from the brainstorming sessions to compile a comprehensive list. Delphi round 2: survey Copenhagen Academy for Medical Education and Simulation Needs Assessment Formula (CAMES-NAF), which was previously used as NAF was used to prioritise procedures. 15 This formula explored four factors: Frequency of the procedure. Doctors requiring training. Training impact on patients. Feasibility of SBT. Each factor was rated on a 5-point scale (1–5). The CAMES-NAF score was calculated as the average of these four factors, with equal weightage of 25% each. Frequency of the procedure in your department: 1=never or few times a year, 2=every month, 3=every week, 4=every day, 5=many times every day. Percentage of the physicians/doctors who should be able to perform the procedure in your department: 1=0%–20%, 2=21%–40%, 3=41%–60%, 4=61%–80%, 5=81%–100%. Agreement with the following statement: ‘This procedure is uncomfortable or risky for the patient if performed by an incompetent doctor’: 1=totally disagree, 2=disagree, 3=neither agree nor disagree, 4=agree, 5=totally agree. Lastly, the feasibility for SBT of a given procedure is determined by the research group during the second round as it was challenging for the participants to estimate. Consequently, the steering group conducted an assessment of feasibility, calculating it as the mean score of three equally weighted factors: Suitability for SBT: Evaluated the appropriateness of the procedure for SBT. Equipment availability: Assessed the accessibility of necessary simulation equipment. Associated costs: Considered the financial implications related to the implementation of SBT. This comprehensive approach ensured a balanced evaluation of feasibility, incorporating both logistical and financial considerations. Delphi round 3: elimination and prioritisation In the third round of the Delphi process, participants were provided with the preliminary prioritised list of technical procedures developed in round 2 and list of scenarios and non-technical skills from round 1. They were tasked with reviewing this list and eliminating procedures they deemed unsuitable for SBT. Criteria for elimination included: Inappropriateness for simulation: Procedures that were not conducive to being taught in a simulated environment. Basic procedures: Simple technical procedures that should have been mastered during undergraduate medical education. Advanced procedures: Highly complex procedures that are typically learnt in the later stages of specialist training. Additionally, participants had the opportunity to reprioritise the remaining procedures based on their relevance and importance for SBT. Data analysis In Delphi round 1, we grouped similar skills together where the procedures could be combined and trained in one training programme. We separated the non-technical skills from the technical and put them in a separate category. We also made a separate category for scenarios. In Delphi round 2, the mean score for each factor was calculated and the CAMES NAF score was used to produce a preliminary list of technical procedures. The qualitative data (open-ended questions) for scenarios and non-technical skills were collected and ranked according to most suggested to least suggested. In Delphi round 3, we calculated the number of occurrences for each response using frequency analysis and applied a degree of agreement of ≥70% to establish consensus. The skills and scenarios that had <70% support from the participants were eliminated from the final list. The final list was developed following a focused group discussion among the steering research members. Results Results of Delphi round 1 The responses from participants were collected and thoroughly evaluated by the steering committee. They were categorised into three categories: 22 technical skills, 10 non-technical skills, 21 scenarios ( table 1 ). Table 1. List of skills and scenarios identified in Delphi round 1. Emergency scenarios Technical skills Non-technical skills 1. Cannot intubate cannot oxygenate 2. Laryngospasm 3. Bronchospasm 4. Aspiration 5. Anaphylaxis 6. Adult cardiac arrest 7. Paediatric cardiac arrest 8. Intraoperative myocardial ischaemia 9. Severe haemorrhage 10. Air/gas embolism 11. Haemolytic transfusion reaction 12. Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity 13. Malignant hyperthermia 14. Hyperkalaemia 15. Fire airway 16. Patient maternal collapse 17. Neonatal collapse 18. Total/high spinal 19. Postpartum haemorrhage 20. Peripartum seizure 21. Amniotic fluid embolism 1. IV placement 2. ECG interpretation 3. ABG interpretation 4. Arterial line insertion 5. Nasogastric/feeding tube insertion 6. Endotracheal intubation insertion of airway adjuncts 8. Emergency surgical airway 9. CPR 10. Paediatric caudal anaesthesia 11. Spinal anaesthesia 12. Epidural catheter insertion 13. Central venous catheterisation 14. Paediatric central venous catheterisation 15. Ultrasound-guided peripheral nerve block techniques 16. Fibre optic bronchoscopy 17. Defibrillation 18. Cardioversion 19. Cardiac pacing 20. Needle decompression of pneumothorax 21. Point of care ultrasound 22. Transoesophageal echocardiography interpretation 1. Communicating with patients regarding informed consent for anaesthesia 2. Communicating with family members during perioperative emergencies 3. Handover communication between anaesthesia providers 4. Communicating with other healthcare team members during crises 5. Disclosing medical errors or adverse events to patients or their families 6. Providing feedback to colleagues in a constructive manner 7. Breaking Bad News with patient or Family 8. Communicating effectively during multidisciplinary/interprofessional team meetings 9. Escalating an emergency to the senior on call 10. Call for help Open in a new tab ABG, Arterial Blood Gas; CPR, Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation. Keywords identified for the curriculum in the first round were ‘practice ready’, ‘confidence’ and ‘better communication’. Based on the responses, the most common challenges in implementing simulation-based curriculum found were: (1) Resource constraints, (2) Organisational and logistical challenges and (3) Simulation design and implementation challenges. Results of Delphi round 2 A total of 90 participants responded in the first round. They were invited to the study for further rounds. But 77 anaesthesiologists responded in the second round, resulting in a response rate of 85.5% (77/90). A preprioritised list of technical procedures was generated from the first round. The second round of the Delphi process requires the respondents to review the list of items that were summarised according to the answers provided in the first round and rate them. Traditionally, the panel is required to review the items and rate or rank-order them based on their overall opinion of each item. In a modified version of the Delphi process, Nayahangan et al developed a NAF using different factors to explore each item in more detail, which we used for the technical skills. 15 Through this, we could narrow down topics that needed attention through SBT ( table 2 ). Table 2. Technical skills rated according to the CAMES-NAF. Procedure/skill Frequency Operators Impact Feasibility NAF score Priority IV placement 5 5 3 5 4.5 High ECG interpretation 5 5 4 1 3.75 Medium ABG 5 5 4 4 4.5 High Arterial line insertion 4 4 3 4 3.75 Medium NG/feeding tube insertion 3 4 3 4 3.5 Medium Endotracheal intubation 5 5 5 5 5.0 High Airway adjuncts insertion 4 5 4 5 4.5 High Emergency surgical airway 1 5 5 4 3.75 Medium CPR 3 5 5 5 4.5 High Paediatric caudal anaesthesia 3 3 4 4 3.5 Medium Spinal anaesthesia 4 4 4 4 4.0 Medium Epidural catheter insertion 4 4 4 4 4.0 Medium CVC 4 4 4 4 4.0 Medium Paediatric CVC 2 3 5 4 3.5 Medium US-guided nerve blocks 4 4 3 4 3.75 Medium Fibre optic bronchoscopy 2 3 4 4 3.25 Low Defibrillation 2 5 5 5 4.25 Medium Cardioversion 2 5 4 5 4.0 Medium Cardiac pacing 2 4 5 4 3.75 Medium Needle decompression 1 5 5 4 3.75 Medium POCUS 4 4 3 4 3.75 Medium TEE interpretation 2 3 4 4 3.25 Low Open in a new tab ABG, Arterial Blood Gas; CAMES-NAF, Copenhagen Academy for Medical Education and Simulation Needs Assessment Formula; CPR, Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation; CVC, central venous catheterisation; NG, nasogastric; POCUS, point of care ultrasound; TEE, transoesophageal echocardiography. Based on the provided responses, the most frequently mentioned skill that individuals would like to practise was endotracheal intubation with NAF score of 5. Airway management was given more importance by participants. This includes intubation, difficult airway management and other techniques to secure the airway. Results of Delphi round 3 The final prioritised list included 8 scenarios, 10 technical skills and 5 non-technical skills after reprioritising and eliminating ( table 3 ). Table 3. Final list of topics for curriculum. Technical skills Scenarios (higher order thinking) Non-technical skills 1. Emergency airway-cricothyrotomy 1. Failed airway 1. Teamwork 2. Ultrasound-guided vascular access including paediatric 2. Circulatory collapse 2. Leadership 3. Needle decompression of pneumothorax 3. Anaphylaxis 3. Communication 4. I–V placement 4. Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity 4. Decision-making 5. Endotracheal tube placement 5. Malignant hyperthermia 5. Situation awareness and risk perception 6. ABG 6. Neonatal collapse 7. Fibreoptic intubation 7. Maternal collapse 8. Defibrillation 8. Airway fire 9. Cardioversion 10. Airway adjuncts Open in a new tab ABG, Arterial Blood Gas. A Spearman’s r of 0.94 (p<0.0001) showed a strong correlation between the prioritisation order of the technical procedures from Delphi round 2 and the final list in Delphi round 3. Discussion Our needs assessment successfully identified and prioritised key technical and non-technical skills and scenarios that should be integrated into a simulation-based curriculum for anaesthesiology residents. The high response rate of anaesthesiologists from 32 institutions and broad representation across different designations—from residents (30.3%) to experienced faculty (28.9% with >15 years of experience)—demonstrates strong support within the field and enhances the generalisability of our findings. Similar to findings in paediatric training, our study identified critical procedural skills that are high-stakes but may have limited exposure during routine training. 16 The top priorities identified in our assessment align with essential competencies in anaesthesiology, particularly focusing on airway management, vascular access and emergency response scenarios. This mirrors the emphasis on core life-saving procedures found in other specialties’ simulation curriculums like that of paediatrics and emergency medicine. 15 16 A notable finding from our study was the strong emphasis on emergency scenarios. Failed airway management, circulatory failure, anaphylaxis, malignant hyperthermia and maternal/fetal collapse emerged as critical scenarios requiring simulation training. This aligns with the growing recognition that high-risk, low-frequency events are particularly well-suited for simulation-based education, as highlighted in recent medical education literature. 17 Hence, the skill like fibreoptic intubation has been added as a skill, although according to CAMES-NAF, it was a low priority. The same goes for local anaesthesia systemic toxicity and malignant hyperthermia, as they can be learnt only by simulation, as it is very rare and comes with a high mortality rate. The identification of non-technical skills, particularly interprofessional and hierarchical communication, as crucial areas for simulation training reflects an evolution in medical education. This finding emphasises that modern anaesthesiology training must go beyond technical proficiency to include essential communication and teamwork skills. 18 19 While achieving a consensus view, findings are still based on professional opinion. The challenges identified in implementing simulation-based curriculum included resource constraints, organisational barriers and technical issues. Resource constraints: Financial: Limited budgets for purchasing equipment, software and hiring skilled personnel. Infrastructure: Lack of dedicated simulation facilities and equipment. Time: Difficulty in allocating time for training, development and conducting simulations. Human resources: Shortage of trained simulation faculty and staff. Organisational and logistical challenges: Resistance to change: Difficulty in convincing faculty and staff about the benefits of SBT. Scheduling conflicts: Competing demands from clinical duties and other academic activities. Technical Issues: Problems with equipment and software. Simulation design and implementation challenges: Scenario development: Creating realistic and engaging scenarios that align with learning objectives. Instructor training: Ensuring that instructors are adequately trained to facilitate effective simulation sessions. Evaluation and feedback: Developing reliable methods to assess learner performance and provide meaningful feedback However, our study provides specific context for these challenges within anaesthesiology training, allowing for more targeted solutions. A key strength of this study is the participation of anaesthesiologists from 32 different colleges, which demonstrates strong support within the field and enhances the generalisability and credibility of the findings. Furthermore, our questionnaire underwent peer review and assessment for face validity, which increases its reliability. The mixed-methods approach, combining quantitative data with qualitative insights, provided a comprehensive understanding of training needs. The use of Google Forms and modern analytical tools, including AI assistance for data interpretation, represents an innovative approach to needs assessment methodology. A noteworthy limitation of this study is that the needs assessment was conducted at a national level, which may pose challenges for direct implementation in countries with differing professional standards and traditions. Looking ahead, it would be valuable to conduct a needs assessment in other countries and compare the results. Participants in this study included alumni and their professional networks, which could raise concerns about potential bias. Given that the study centred on anaesthesiology practices and skills, the affiliation with the institution was unlikely to introduce bias in their responses. While participation bias is an inherent aspect of research involving human subjects, efforts were made to mitigate its impact by ensuring a diverse and representative sample of participants. Alumni and their professional networks were used for their accessibility and relevance to the research focus. Practical implications Our findings have immediate practical implications for anaesthesiology education: The prioritised list of technical skills provides a framework for developing structured simulation modules. The identified non-technical skills suggest the need for integrated scenarios that combine procedural and communication elements. The challenges identified can inform resource allocation and implementation strategies. The strong emphasis on emergency scenarios suggests the need for dedicated crisis management simulation modules. Future directions Moving forward, this needs assessment can serve as a foundation for: Developing standardised simulation modules aligned with identified priorities. Creating assessment tools for competency evaluation. Establishing guidelines for minimum simulation training requirements. Designing faculty development programmes to support simulation-based education. Conclusions This needs assessment successfully identified and prioritised key areas for SBT in anaesthesiology. The findings provide a framework for developing comprehensive simulation curriculum that addresses both technical and non-technical skills. By focusing on high-priority procedures and scenarios, training programmes can optimise resource utilisation while ensuring residents develop essential competencies in a safe learning environment. Footnotes Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Ethics approval: This study involves human participants and was approved by the Institutional Ethical Committee (Ref No: FMIEC/CCM 1383 12024). Participants gave informed consent to participate in the study before taking part. Patient and public involvement: Patients and/or the public were not involved in the design, or conduct, or reporting, or dissemination plans of this research. Supplementary material Data availability statement All data relevant to the study are included in the article or uploaded as supplementary information. References 1. Issenberg SB, Ringsted C, Ostergaard D, et al. Setting a research agenda for simulation-based healthcare education: a synthesis of the outcome from an Utstein style meeting. Simul Healthc. 2011;6:155–67. doi: 10.1097/SIH.0b013e3182207c24. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Pande A, Muthiah T, Ramachandran R, et al. Integration of simulation-based teaching in anaesthesiology curriculum. Indian J Anaesth. 2024;68:24–30. doi: 10.4103/ija.ija_1254_23. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Romero P, Günther P, Kowalewski K-F, et al. Halsted’s “See One, Do One, and Teach One” versus Peyton’s Four-Step Approach: A Randomized Trial for Training of Laparoscopic Suturing and Knot Tying. J Surg Educ. 2018;75:510–5. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2017.07.025. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kneebone RL. Simulation in surgical training: educational issues and practical implications. Med Educ. 2003;37:267–77. doi: 10.1046/j.1365-2923.2003.01440.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. NMC Competency-based postgraduate training programme guidelines for residents in anaesthesiology. 2019 6. Savoldelli GL, Burlacu CL, Lazarovici M, et al. Integration of simulation-based education in anaesthesiology specialist training: Synthesis of results from an Utstein Meeting. Eur J Anaesthesiol. 2024;41:43–54. doi: 10.1097/EJA.0000000000001913. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. McGaghie WC, Bordage G, Telder TV. The effect of simulation on learning outcomes in medical education: a systematic review. Med Educ. 2010;44:225–39. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Al-Shidhani TA. Curriculum Development for Medical Education : A Six-Step Approach. Sultan Qaboos Univ Med J. 2010;10:416–7. doi: 10.18295/2075-0528.1231. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Gustavsen PH, Nielsen DG, Paltved C, et al. A national needs assessment study to determine procedures for simulation-based training in cardiology in Denmark. Scand Cardiovasc J. 2019;53:35–41. doi: 10.1080/14017431.2019.1569716. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Thomsen ASS, la Cour M, Paltved C, et al. Consensus on procedures to include in a simulation-based curriculum in ophthalmology: a national Delphi study. Acta Ophthalmol. 2018;96:519–27. doi: 10.1111/aos.13700. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Nayahangan LJ, Konge L, Schroeder TV, et al. A National Needs Assessment to Identify Technical Procedures in Vascular Surgery for Simulation Based Training. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg. 2017;53:591–9. doi: 10.1016/j.ejvs.2017.01.011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Nayahangan LJ, Bølling Hansen R, Gilboe Lindorff-Larsen K, et al. Identifying content for simulation-based curricula in urology: a national needs assessment. Scand J Urol. 2017;51:484–90. doi: 10.1080/21681805.2017.1352618. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Nayahangan LJ, Clementsen PF, Paltved C, et al. Identifying Technical Procedures in Pulmonary Medicine That Should Be Integrated in a Simulation-Based Curriculum: A National General Needs Assessment. Respiration. 2016;91:517–22. doi: 10.1159/000446926. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Nayahangan LJ, Nielsen KR, Albrecht-Beste E, et al. Determining procedures for simulation-based training in radiology: a nationwide needs assessment. Eur Radiol. 2018;28:2319–27. doi: 10.1007/s00330-017-5244-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Nayahangan LJ, Stefanidis D, Kern DE, et al. How to identify and prioritize procedures suitable for simulation-based training: Experiences from general needs assessments using a modified Delphi method and a needs assessment formula. Med Teach. 2018;40:676–83. doi: 10.1080/0142159X.2018.1472756. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Thim S, Nayahangan LJ, Paltved C, et al. Identifying and prioritising technical procedures for simulation-based curriculum in paediatrics: a Delphi-based general needs assessment. BMJ Paediatr Open . 2020;4:e000697. doi: 10.1136/bmjpo-2020-000697. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Veenstra BR, Wojtowicz A, Walsh N, et al. The emergency surgical airway: Bridging the gap from quality outcome to performance improvement through a novel simulation based curriculum. Am J Surg. 2019;217:562–5. doi: 10.1016/j.amjsurg.2018.09.026. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Fletcher GCL, McGeorge P, Flin RH, et al. The role of non-technical skills in anaesthesia: a review of current literature. Br J Anaesth. 2002;88:418–29. doi: 10.1093/bja/88.3.418. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Hughes T, McGowan A, O’Neill S, et al. Developing a curriculum in anaesthesia: the role of non-technical skills. Anaesthesia. 2018;73:787–94. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement All data relevant to the study are included in the article o