Scholar ] 36. Robba C, Ortu A, Bilotta F, et al. Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation for adult respiratory distress syndrome in trauma patients: a case series and systematic literature review. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2017;82(1):165–173. doi: 10.1097/TA.0000000000001276. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary material 1 (DOCX 4632 kb) (4.5MB, docx)',32,'2025-10-15 10:59:29','2025-10-15 10:59:29');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5143,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2658283','Abstract-to-publication ratio for papers presented at scientific meetings: a quality marker for UK emergency medicine research.','Macmillan CD, Moore AK, Cook RJ, Pedley DK.','2007','walby bimonthly guarded publishable walby bunney 20018a582 oderda walby georgakas korn tintinalli perish callaham wears','Abstract Objectives To determine the publication rate of abstracts presented by UK emergency physicians at major emergency medicine meetings, and to identify the site of publication of papers. Method All abstracts presented to the annual scientific meetings of both the British Association of Emergency Medicine and the Faculty of Accident and Emergency Medicine between 2001 and 2002 were identified retrospectively from conference programmes. To identify whether the work relating to the abstract had been published in a peer‐reviewed journal, the Medline database (Ovid interface) was searched using the first and last authors as well as key words from the abstract. Results Of the 404 abstracts identified, 124 (30%) had been published as full articles. For abstracts presented in the oral sessions, 83 (57%) resulted in publication. A range of journals accepted papers for publication. Conclusion The abstract‐to‐publication ratio for UK emergency medicine is lower than for other specialties, but broadly similar to emergency medicine in the US and Australia. Communication of information within a profession requires a forum via which it can be transmitted, and within medicine this is commonly at scientific meetings, as poster or oral presentations. Presentations may be made to describe and evidence new practice, to present research in a public forum or to draw attention to an area of interest. Many trainees in medical specialties are encouraged to undertake oral or poster presentations as part of their training. 1 It has been proposed that a marker of the quality of a presentation is in its realisation, or not, as a publication in a peer‐reviewed journal; consequently, it has been suggested that the quality of research output from a scientific body or specialty may be judged by how many abstracts are subsequently published. 2 Walby et al 3 used abstract‐to‐publication ratios (A:P) ratios to consider the quality of Australasian research output, and compared it with other specialties and with US emergency medicine. To date, however, there has been no such analysis of the output from emergency medicine within the UK. The aims of this study were to assess the A:P ratio for poster and oral presentations from recent emergency medicine conferences, and to compare it with other specialties and with emergency medicine elsewhere. The sites of publication were also collated. Method Using conference programmes for the British Association of Emergency Medicine and the Faculty of Accident and Emergency Medicine, we identified abstracts presented at those conferences in 2001 and 2002. These included posters and oral presentations. The programmes detailed the title and authors and differentiated posters from oral presentations. To assess whether the abstract had been published, key words from the title and first and last authors were used for search purposes; first authors were used as they were considered to have written the paper, and last authors were used as they are most likely to be senior and therefore be named on any final paper. These title and author details were entered into a search of Medline (Ovid interface) from 2001 to December 2005. We judged that a 3‐year period should be adequate for the completion and publication of information presented. Date of publication, journal of publication and whether the abstract was originally a poster or presentation were recorded; Excel spreadsheets were used to record the data. Results A total of 404 abstracts from four conferences were identified; of these, 124 (30%) had been published. For abstracts presented in the oral sessions, 83 (57%) resulted in publication; only 41 (14%) of the poster presentations resulted in publication. Papers were published in a variety of journals. The Emergency Medical Journal was the most common site of publication, having published 65 (53%) papers; the next most common site of publication was the European Journal of Emergency Medicine, with 10 (8%) publications. A wide range of other journals carried publications including Resuscitation (2%), Injury (2%) and the Journal of Trauma (2%). The time interval from abstract to publication ranged from 4 months to 4 years. Discussion Calculating the abstract to publication ratio may be useful in judging the quantity and quality of research within our specialty. As a marker, the abstract to publication ratio may allow us to assess our current research status and compare it with international emergency medicine and with other specialties. Furthermore, establishing a baseline abstract to publication ratio now sets a benchmark for UK emergency medicine conferences. However, for a temporal comparison of the abstract to publication ratio to be valid, the “publishing environment” must remain similar. It is worth noting that the Emergency Medical Journal was a bimonthly publication during the time frame considered, with an inevitable impact on available copy and delay in publication. Similarly, international comparison and comparison between specialties need to be guarded as peer‐review processes and thresholds for publication differ in these different publishing environments. These results suggest that emergency medicine in the UK in recent years has produced research to a standard similar to that in the US (23–47%) 4 and to that in Australia (35%). 3 However, in comparison with other specialties within the UK, emergency medicine would seem to produce a lower percentage of publishable research (fig 1). This may represent a poorer quality of research, but may also reflect the more unpredictable nature of the caseload and what is traditionally perceived to be a less scientific field. 5 Weber et al 6 explored the reasons for authors having failed to publish their work in a survey of 183 abstracts presented to the 1991 meeting of the Society for Academic Emergency Medicine. Of the responders, 42% identified time pressure and failing to write the manuscript, rather than rejection during the peer‐review process. Figure 1 Percentage of research published in other specialties (with permission from Walby et al 3 ). GP, general practice; A/E, accident and emergency. Open in a new tab We chose not to contact authors directly as part of our study, because previous experience had shown response rates to be disappointing. We considered publication in a Medline‐cited journal to be the standard for publication. It is possible that authors may have published their work in non‐cited journals, in journals represented on other databases or that authors other than those presenting the work eventually published the data. This may have led to an underestimation of publication rates. Conclusion UK emergency medicine compares well internationally within its own specialty but less well with other specialties; emergency physicians and trainees now have a baseline against which future research output can be compared. We hope that this analysis encourages an improved research output from emergency medicine within the UK. Footnotes Funding: None. Competing interests: None. References 1. Sloan E P, Bunney B, Silva J. et al A structured process for fulfilling resident research requirement. Acad Emerg Med 20018A582 [ Google Scholar ] 2. Gorman R L, Oderda G M. Publication of presented abstracts at scientific meetings: a marker of quality? Vet Hum Toxicol 199032470–472. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Walby A, Kelly A, Georgakas C. Abstract to publication ratio for papers presented at scientific meetings: how does emergency medicine compare? Emerg Med 200113460–464. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Korn C S, Henderson S O. Publication of research abstracts presented at four emergency medicine research forums. Acad Emerg Med 20007534 [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tintinalli J. Publish or perish: easier said than done. Emerg Med 200113407–408. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Weber E, Callaham M, Wears R L. et al Unpublished research from a medical specialty meeting: why investigators fail to publish. JAMA 1998280257–259. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',23,'2025-10-15 10:59:30','2025-10-15 10:59:30');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5144,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11059256','Profiles of Lifestyle Health Behaviors and Postmortem Dementia-Related Neuropathology.','Lange-Maia BS, Wagner M, Rogers CA, Mehta RI, Bennett DA, Tangney C, Schoeny ME, Halloway S, Arvanitakis Z.','2024','enrolls gardening yard calisthenics bicycling restaurants teletract bingo magazines checkers puzzles ffq ffq estimate_profiles mclust tidylpa lpas socializers nonsmokers gingko glae100_suppl_supplementary_materials glae100_suppl_supplementary_materials 163kb altruism traci colvin greg brittney maude rupal christy tangney schoeny halloway biobehavioral zoe gustavo duque r01ns084965 rf1ag059621 rf1ag074549 rf1ag076124 r01ag017917 r01nr018443 p30ag010161 p30ag072975 huntley sommerlad s1355617711000531 hoganson tangney slows 0b013e3182535d35 halloway tangney dawe e1911 e1919 0b013e31829c5e8a e2259 e2268 fleischman vemuri lesnick przybelski byun kbase nyas hillman stillman dingle bowe bujtor moored bandeen snitz geronb gbab152 glae100_suppl_supplementary_materials glae100_suppl_supplementary_materials 163kb','Abstract High engagement in lifestyle health behaviors appears to be protective against cognitive decline in aging. We investigated the association between patterns of modifiable lifestyle health behaviors and common brain neuropathologies of dementia as a possible mechanism. We examined 555 decedents from the Rush Memory and Aging Project, free of dementia at their initial concurrent report of lifestyle health behaviors of interest (physical, social, and cognitive activities, and healthy diet), and who underwent a postmortem neuropathology evaluation. First, we used latent profile analysis to group participants based on baseline behavior patterns. Second, we assessed the associations of profile membership with each neurodegenerative (global Alzheimer’s disease [AD] pathology, amyloid-beta load, density of neurofibrillary tangles, and presence of cortical Lewy bodies and TAR DNA-binding protein 43 cytoplasmic inclusions) and neurovascular pathologies (presence of chronic gross or microscopic infarcts, arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, and cerebral amyloid angiopathy), using separate linear or logistic regression models, adjusted for age at death, sex (core model), vascular disease risk factors, and vascular conditions (fully adjusted model). Participants had either consistently lower ( N = 224) or consistently higher ( N = 331) engagement across 4 lifestyle health behaviors. We generally found no differences in neuropathologies between higher and lower engagement groups in core or fully adjusted models; for example, higher engagement in lifestyle health behaviors was not associated with global AD pathology after core or full adjustment (both p > .8). In conclusion, we found no evidence of associations between patterns of lifestyle health behaviors and neuropathology. Other mechanisms may underlie protective effects of health behaviors against dementia. Keywords: Dementia, Lifestyle, Neurodegeneration, Neuropathology, Vascular Alzheimer’s disease (AD) and Alzheimer’s disease-related dementias (ADRD) are among the leading causes of morbidity and chronic disability in older adults ( 1 ). Though significant advancements in our understanding of AD have been made in recent decades, few AD disease-modifying treatments exist, and the public health and financial burden of AD/ADRD is exponentially rising ( 1 ). Currently, no established preventive approaches exist for AD/ADRD. However, a growing body of research suggests that modifiable lifestyle factors may play important roles by slowing cognitive decline and reducing the risk of dementia, the principal clinical manifestations of AD/ADRD ( 2 ). Though many studies examine links between lifestyle health behaviors and cognitive decline, considerably fewer have investigated potential biological mechanisms of these behaviors, with scant research on the association between lifestyle health behaviors and neuropathology, particularly using human tissues. We previously reported that engagement in lifestyle health behaviors of physical, social, and cognitive activities and adherence to a healthy diet (specifically, the Mediterranean-DASH Diet Intervention for Neurodegenerative Delay [MIND] diet) are each associated with higher levels of cognition and slower cognitive decline in the Rush Memory and Aging Project (MAP), a prospective community-based study of aging ( 3–6 ). Further, we recently demonstrated that higher engagement across these lifestyle health behaviors as a group is associated with slower cognitive decline ( 7 ). We have also investigated associations between individual lifestyle health behaviors proximal to death with neuropathologies among MAP decedents, though these relationships are less clear. As examples, nigral neuronal loss, macroinfarcts, and white matter pathologies are associated with lower physical activity ( 8 ). Higher social engagement proximal to death appears to confer protective effects against cognitive deterioration among persons with neurodegenerative pathologies ( 9 ). More frequent cognitive activity is associated with less cognitive decline, independent of neuropathologic burden, suggesting roles in cognitive reserve ( 10 ). Finally, MIND diet adherence is associated with less postmortem AD pathology, primarily β-amyloid load, and may be associated with better cognition independent of AD and other common age-related brain neuropathologies ( 11 ). However, the combined engagement in multiple behaviors simultaneously has not been studied in relation to neuropathology. Here, we extend our previous work exploring profiles of lifestyle health behavior in association with cognitive decline ( 7 ) by studying whether differences exist in the presence and levels of common degenerative and vascular brain neuropathologies, associated with dementia among older persons, with different profiles of lifestyle health behaviors in the years prior to death. Overall, the goal of this work is to better understand the potential underlying biology of cognitive differences related to concurrent lifestyle health behaviors. Method Study Participants The MAP is an ongoing longitudinal clinical-pathologic study of aging and dementia, which started in 1997 and enrolls older persons from more than 40 retirement communities and housing facilities in and near Chicago, IL, USA ( 12 ). Eligibility requires the absence of known dementia at time of enrollment and agreement to annual in-home clinical evaluations and brain donation at death. To date, the follow-up rate exceeds 90%, and the autopsy rate exceeds 80%. All participants signed an informed consent and an Anatomical Gift Act for organ donation, and the study protocol was approved by the Rush University Medical Center Institutional Review Board. Analytic Sample Of the 2 297 MAP participants, 1 165 were deceased, and 695 individuals participated in the dietary substudy launched in 2004. We excluded 1 participant without concurrent data on the 4 lifestyle health behaviors of interest, 47 with a clinical diagnosis of dementia at the first assessment of all health behaviors (analytical baseline), and 92 missing data on global AD pathology, resulting in an analytical sample of 555 participants. Lifestyle Health Behaviors Lifestyle health behaviors were assessed through questionnaires ( 12 ). A 5-item physical activity questionnaire summarized hours per week spent on common physical activities (such as walking for exercise, gardening/yard work, calisthenics/general exercise, bicycling, or swimming/water exercises) ( 13 ). A 6-item questionnaire summarized the frequency of participating in common activities involving social interaction (going to restaurants, sporting events, teletract or playing bingo; going on day or overnight trips; doing unpaid community/volunteer work; visiting relative or friends’ houses; participating in social groups; or attending church or religious services) ( 14 ). Additionally, a 7-item late-life cognitive activity questionnaire summarized the frequency of engaging in cognitively stimulating activities in the past year (total daily reading; visiting a library; reading newspapers, magazines, and books; writing letters; playing games like checkers, board games, cards, or puzzles) ( 4 ). Social and cognitive activity item scores range from 1 (once/year or less) to 5 (every day) and are averaged to yield total scores from 1 to 5. Dietary habits (usual intake over the past year) were assessed using a 144-item semiquantitative food frequency questionnaire (FFQ) that was modified from the Willett FFQ to be suitable for older adults ( 5 ). A healthy diet score was calculated based on adherence to the MIND diet (range 0–15, higher scores indicate greater adherence), which has been associated with better late-life cognitive health ( 5 ). Postmortem Neuropathologic Outcomes Details of the postmortem evaluations have been previously reported ( 15 , 16 ). Briefly, brain autopsies were performed following uniform procedures, including details regarding gross examination, regions of interest, and immunohistochemistry. Neuropathological examinations yielded measures of degenerative and vascular pathologies. Degenerative pathologies included a global score for AD pathology, global amyloid-beta load, and neurofibrillary tangle density (both based on immunohistochemical methods with image analyses, from which we derived continuous measures indicating levels of pathology), as well as the presence of cortical Lewy bodies and TAR DNA-binding protein 43 [TDP-43] cytoplasmic inclusions ( 16 , 17 ). Vascular pathologies (all binary variables) included the presence of infarcts (any chronic, gross chronic, or microscopic infarcts) and the presence of moderate or severe (vs none or mild) vessel pathologies of arteriolosclerosis, atherosclerosis, and cerebral amyloid angiopathy (CAA) ( 18 , 19 ). Covariates Covariates included age at analytical baseline for descriptive purposes and at death for analyses, sex, number of self-reported vascular risk factors (sum of hypertension, diabetes, and current/former smoking), and number of self-reported vascular conditions (sum of claudication, heart conditions, congestive heart failure, and stroke [also determined from neurologic evaluation]). Statistical Analysis First, to identify distinct groups of engagement in lifestyle health behaviors at analytical baseline, we conducted latent profile analysis (LPA), a statistical method that allows for identifying unmeasured profile membership from a given set of continuous variables, using the 4 continuous lifestyle health behaviors, as previously applied in a larger MAP sample ( 7 ) (more details on the LPA have been provided in Supplementary Methods ). Second, we tested the hypothesis that healthier lifestyle behaviors at analytical baseline were associated with lower levels of pathology. To do so, separate linear regression models were employed to test the associations between the groups identified through LPA and each continuous neuropathology outcome, specifically global AD pathology, amyloid-beta load, and neurofibrillary tangle density. In addition, separate logistic regression models were applied to examine the association between group membership and the presence of cortical Lewy bodies, TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions, infarcts, moderate/severe arteriosclerosis, atherosclerosis, and amyloid angiopathy (each compared to none/mild severity). Core models were adjusted for age at death and sex. In fully adjusted models, we then added the number of vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, smoking history) and the number of vascular conditions (claudication, stroke, heart conditions, congestive heart failure) assessed at analytical baseline. Lastly, in sensitivity analyses, to account for varied length of follow-up between analytical baseline and death, we repeated the linear regression models with a term added for this timeframe. All statistical analyses were conducted using R software version 4.0.3 (R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria), and the estimate_profiles function, using mclust models of tidyLPA R package version 1.0.8., was used for the LPAs. Results At analytical baseline, the 555 participants had an average age of 84 years (standard deviation [ SD ] = 6) and 73% of the sample was female. Two latent profiles representing distinct patterns of lifestyle health behavior engagement were identified: a group that had consistent lower engagement across behaviors ( N = 224), and a group with consistently higher engagement ( N = 331). LPA fit statistics are provided in Supplementary Table S1 , as are post hoc comparisons between the 2- and 3-profile solution ( Supplementary Figure S1 ). Mean levels of engagement were different between groups for all behaviors ( p < .001 for each; means listed in Table 1 , graphic depiction of differences in Figure 1 ). The 2 groups did not differ by age at analytical baseline or death, or by sex. However, the higher engagement group has a smaller number of vascular disease risk factors and vascular disease conditions, on average ( Table 1 ). Bivariate statistics regarding presence of degenerative or vascular pathologies did not differ between the higher and lower engagement groups ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Characteristics of Decedents According to the Profiles of Lifestyle Health Behavior Engagement, Memory, and Aging Project All ( N = 555) Consistently Low Engagement ( n = 224) Consistently High Engagement ( n = 331) p Value * Demographics Mean age at analytical baseline ( SD ), years 84 (6) 85 (6) 84 (75) .1 Mean age at death ( SD ), years 92 (6) 91 (7) 92 (6) .1 Female sex, n (%) 405 (73) 159 (71) 248 (75) .7 Mean follow-up ( SD ), years 7.5 (4.0) 6.4 (4.0) 8.3 (4.0) <.0001 Mean number of vascular disease risk factors ( SD ) † 1.1 (0.8) 1.2 (0.8) 1.0 (0.8) .002 Mean number of vascular disease conditions ( SD ) ‡ 0.4 (0.7) 0.5 (0.8) 0.4 (0.6) .002 Lifestyle health behaviors at analytical baseline Mean physical activity ( SD ) (log-transformed) § 0.80 (0.9) 0.37 (0.8) 1.09 (0.8) <.0001 Mean social activity score ( SD ) (possible range 1–5) 2.6 (0.6) 2.2 (0.5) 2.8 (0.5) <.0001 Mean cognitive activity score ( SD ) (possible range 1–5) 3.2 (0.7) 2.7 (0.6) 3.6 (0.5) <.0001 Mean MIND diet score ( SD ) (possible range 0–15) 7.6 (1.6) 6.6 (1.3) 8.4 (1.4) <.0001 Neuropathology at death Degenerative pathology Mean global AD pathology ( SD ) 0.71 (0.6) 0.69 (0.6) 0.72 (0.6) .6 Mean amyloid-beta load ( SD ) 4.50 (4.4) 4.60 (4.4) 4.43 (4.3) .7 Mean neurofibrillary tangle density ( SD ) 7.18 (8.3) 6.49 (7.4) 7.67 (8.8) .1 Presence of cortical Lewy bodies, n (%) 78 (14) 29 (13) 50 (15) .5 Presence of TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions, n (%) 200 (36) 74 (33) 126 (38) .2 Vascular pathology, n (%) Presence of gross infarcts 211 (38) 90 (40) 122 (37) .5 Presence of microscopic infarcts 189 (34) 72 (32) 116 (35) .6 Moderate/severe arteriolosclerosis 155 (28) 67 (30) 89 (27) .4 Moderate/severe atherosclerosis 144 (26) 65 (29) 76 (23) .1 Moderate/severe CAA 189 (34) 78 (35) 109 (33) .7 Open in a new tab Notes : CAA = cerebral amyloid angiopathy; MIND = Mediterranean-DASH intervention for neurodegenerative delay; SD = standard deviation; TDP-43 = TAR DNA-binding protein 43. * ANOVA test for quantitative variables, chi-test for binary variable. † Hypertension, diabetes, and smoking history. ‡ Claudication, stroke, heart conditions, and congestive heart failure. § Physical activity levels were log-transformed to reduce skewness of the distribution. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Mean standardized values and 95% confidence intervals of lifestyle health behaviors in the two selected profiles of engagement at baseline; Memory and Aging Project ( N = 555). In core linear and logistic regression models (as appropriate) adjusting for age at death and sex, we found no association between lifestyle health behavior engagement with any of the degenerative or vascular pathologies ( Table 2 ). No significant associations were evident in models further adjusted for vascular risk factors and vascular conditions ( Table 2 ). In sensitivity analyses, results remained similar when the models were additionally adjusted for the length of follow-up (see Supplementary Table S2 ). Table 2. Multivariate-Adjusted Associations Between Lifestyle Heath Behavior Engagement (Reference = Low Engagement) and Brain Degenerative and Vascular Neuropathologies; Memory and Aging Project ( N = 555) Degenerative Pathologies: AD Core Model * Fully Adjusted Model † Mean Difference (95% CI) p Value Mean Difference (95% CI) p Value Global AD pathology 0.02 (−0.08, 0.11) .8 0.01 (−0.09, 0.11) .8 Amyloid-beta load −0.04 (−0.25, 0.17) .7 −0.03 (−0.24, 0.18) .8 Tau neurofibrillary tangles 0.16 (−0.07, 0.38) .2 0.11 (−0.12, 0.34) .4 Degenerative Pathologies: Other OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value Presence of cortical Lewy bodies 1.22 (0.73, 2.06) .4 1.16 (0.70, 1.97) .6 Presence of TDP-43 cytoplasmic inclusions 1.21 (0.84, 1.77) .3 1.18 (0.81, 1.72) .4 Vascular Pathologies OR (95% CI) p Value OR (95% CI) p Value Presence of any chronic infarct 0.92 (0.64, 1.30) .6 0.97 (0.68, 1.39) .9 Presence of gross infarcts 0.86 (0.60, 1.22) .4 0.94 (0.65, 1.36) .8 Presence of microinfarcts 1.09 (0.75, 1.58) .7 1.08 (0.74, 1.57) .7 Moderate/severe arteriolosclerosis 0.78 (0.53, 1.15) .2 0.79 (0.53, 1.17) .2 Moderate/severe atherosclerosis 0.71 (0.48, 1.05) .1 0.78 (0.52, 1.16) .2 Moderate/severe CAA 0.90 (0.62, 1.30) .6 0.86 (0.59, 1.24) .4 Open in a new tab Notes : AD = Alzheimer’s disease; CAA = cerebral amyloid angiopathy; CI = confidence interval; TDP-43 = TAR DNA-binding protein 43. * Each estimate is from a separate linear or logistic regression model adjusted for age at death (continuous, in years) and sex. † Additionally adjusted for number of vascular disease risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, smoking history) and number of vascular disease conditions (claudication, stroke, heart conditions, congestive heart failure) at analytical baseline. Discussion Though we and others have previously demonstrated that higher engagement in lifestyle health behaviors has a protective association against cognitive decline, when examining neuropathologies among decedents who underwent brain autopsy, there was no difference in common degenerative or vascular neuropathologies of dementia between those with higher and those with lower engagement in lifestyle health behaviors. These results suggest that other mechanisms, whether engagement in lifestyle health behaviors for longer periods of time or at earlier stages in life, ApoE genotype, other physiological or neuropathologic changes, or otherwise, may explain the link between lifestyle health behaviors and protection against cognitive decline. Overall, there is a paucity of research focusing on mechanisms linking lifestyle health behaviors with cognition, including the plausible role of neuropathology. Prior work based on the Mayo Clinic Study of Aging ( N = 515; 428 cognitively normal persons and 87 with mild cognitive impairment) examined cross-sectional associations between physical and cognitive activity participation with AD biomarkers quantified through positron emission tomography [PET] and structural magnetic resonance imaging [MRI] ( 20 ). They found that although higher engagement in physical and cognitive activity was associated with better global cognition, there were no significant associations between physical and cognitive activity engagement with the biomarkers studied. The Korean Brain Aging Study for Early Diagnosis and Prediction of AD examined physical activity (in mid- and late-life) and brain amyloid-β (Aβ) deposition ascertained by Pittsburgh Compound B PET, along with AD-signature region cerebral glucose metabolism and cortical thickness on brain MRI ( N = 260; 195 cognitively normal persons and 65 with mild cognitive impairment) ( 21 ). This study found that physical activity moderated the association between Aβ deposition and MRI measures of glucose metabolism and cortical thickness among those with lower physical activity, but not higher, suggesting a potential threshold effect. Taken together, these results suggest that at least some lifestyle behaviors may delay cognitive function but only in certain groups or circumstances. Indeed, impacts on neuropathology may be limited to specific subgroups of older persons, such as those with other vascular risk factors. Yet, in our study, we did not find evidence for this. Overall, the mechanisms underlying potential benefits of healthy lifestyle on cognition are not well understood, and there is inconsistency between animal studies, in vivo imaging, and neuropathological studies. Our study, though comprehensive in the neuropathological assessments and inclusion of multiple health behaviors, adds to the literature showing null associations when examining postmortem pathologies. Alternative mechanisms may include reduction in inflammation, immune signaling, and/or oxidative stress as well as alterations in metabolic and hormonal regulation, including specifically insulin/glucose signaling, sleep, or other neurological pathways not assessed herein ( 22 , 23 ). We opted to include lifestyle behaviors that could be integrated into daily life through community-based interventions with little or no medical intervention. Modifying these behaviors would involve encouraging increases, rather than cessation (like smoking) or discouraging/reducing (like alcohol use), which would potentially necessitate different behavioral strategies. Different combinations of health behaviors and their relationships with cognitive outcomes have been noted in the literature. Latent class analysis was applied to data from a cohort of Australian women to identify distinct lifestyle groupings based upon physical activity, social engagement, alcohol consumption, and smoking. The group characterized as “inactive and low socializers” had a higher risk of dementia compared to those characterized as “highly social nonsmokers” and “highly social, smokers, and drinkers” ( 24 ). Additionally, a secondary analysis of participants in the Gingko Evaluation of Memory Study also used latent class analysis to characterize participation in physical, intellectual/creative, and social activities. Overall, groups characterized by high engagement in a variety of activities and high engagement in intellectual activities had lower risk of dementia compared to those in the group categorized by the lowest engagement across activities ( 25 ). However, in neither report were pathophysiologic mechanisms of these relationships investigated. Strengths of this study include the large number of participants from a well-characterized cohort with a high autopsy rate and systematic pathologic evaluations blinded to clinical data. The range of neuropathologies included allowed us to examine a variety of potential mechanisms, both through several common degenerative and vascular pathologies of aging and dementia. The inclusion of multiple health behaviors, which can be targeted through scalable behavioral interventions is also a strength. Finally, the use of LPA allowed us to identify patterns of engagement in behaviors. Limitations include that the cohort is predominantly female and of White race, limiting the generalizability of our findings. Further, we did not analyze other demographic or genetic data, and we may not have found associations between behaviors and neuropathology if we did not assess the specific measures involved (whether it be behaviors or other neuropathologies) or have yet to identify such measures. These, and other possibilities, remain to be explored. In conclusion, though we and others have previously established associations between higher engagement in lifestyle health behaviors and better cognitive function and slower cognitive decline, here we found no evidence for differences in traditional postmortem neuropathologies of dementia based upon level of engagement in lifestyle health behaviors. Results suggest that mechanisms not assessed in this study are involved in linking lifestyle health behaviors and protection against cognitive decline. Supplementary Material glae100_suppl_Supplementary_Materials glae100_suppl_supplementary_materials.docx (163KB, docx) Acknowledgments The authors are grateful for the altruism of the participants of the Rush Memory and Aging Project. The authors also thank the numerous study staff members, including Traci Colvin, MPH, the study manager, as well as John Gibbons, MS, and Greg Klein, MS, for data management. Contributor Information Brittney S Lange-Maia, Rush Alzheimer’s Disease Center, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Maude Wagner, Rush Alzheimer’s Disease Center, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Christina A Rogers, Rush Medical College, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Rupal I Mehta, Rush Alzheimer’s Disease Center, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. David A Bennett, Rush Alzheimer’s Disease Center, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Christy Tangney, Department of Clinical Nutrition, Rush College of Health Sciences, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Michael E Schoeny, Department of Community, Systems, and Mental Health Nursing, Rush University College of Nursing, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Shannon Halloway, Department of Biobehavioral Nursing Science, College of Nursing, University of Illinois Chicago, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Zoe Arvanitakis, Rush Alzheimer’s Disease Center, Rush University Medical Center, Chicago, Illinois, USA. Gustavo Duque, (Biological Sciences Section). Funding This study was funded by an institutional grant received from Rush University to authors S.H., Z.A., M.E.S., and C.T. (Multiple Principal Investigators). Additionally, this work was supported by the National Institutes of Health [grants R01NS084965, RF1AG059621, RF1AG074549, RF1AG076124, R01AG017917, R01NR018443, P30AG010161, and P30AG072975]. The funding organizations had no role in the design or conduct of the study; the collection, management, analysis, or interpretation of the data; or the writing of the report or the decision to submit it for publication. Conflict of Interest None. Author Contributions Study concept and design: Z.A., S.H., M.E.S., and C.T. Acquisition of data : D.A.B. Data analysis and visualization: M.W. Interpretation of data: all authors. Drafting the manuscript: B.S.L.-M., C.A.R., and Z.A. Critical revision of the manuscript for important intellectual content: all authors. Approval of final manuscript: all authors. References 1. 2023 Alzheimer’s disease facts and figures. Alzheimers Dement 2023;19(4):1598–1695. 10.1002/alz.13016 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Livingston G, Huntley J, Sommerlad A, et al. Dementia prevention, intervention, and care: 2020 report of the Lancet Commission. Lancet. 2020;396(10248):413–446. 10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30367-6 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. James BD, Wilson RS, Barnes LL, Bennett DA.. Late-life social activity and cognitive decline in old age. J Int Neuropsychol Soc. 2011;17(6):998–1005. 10.1017/S1355617711000531 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Wilson RS, Barnes LL, Krueger KR, Hoganson G, Bienias JL, Bennett DA.. Early and late life cognitive activity and cognitive systems in old age. J Int Neuropsychol Soc. 2005;11(4):400–407. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Morris MC, Tangney CC, Wang Y, et al. MIND diet slows cognitive decline with aging. Alzheimers Dement 2015;11(9):1015–1022. 10.1016/j.jalz.2015.04.011 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Buchman AS, Boyle PA, Yu L, Shah RC, Wilson RS, Bennett DA.. Total daily physical activity and the risk of AD and cognitive decline in older