INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5146,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10293097','The Knowledge Gap: Mentorship in Emergency Medicine Residency.','Sobel J, Hayden SR, Wardi G.','2023','nonmentored relevancy relevancy hierarchal etymology odysseus telemachus nonmentored relevancy dismissal faults boosts keyser creighton creighton aaem wiems awaem jedi aaem gwims amwa amwa feminem feminem wim wim aaip aaip ncapip ncapip nhma nhma unfairly rewarded morehouse hone pololi hierarchal onus nondyadic hierarchal pololi deferring confuse preidentified institutionally nondyadic jake toy heiner uhs socal temecula temecula wardi jolla garmel waeckerle perina keyes pilcher piva wagenen lomis dekhtyar zakus flexman sambunjak marusic thurgur hilsabeck keyser lakoski cinisomo 0b13e318163700a nuth stiell s1481803500012173 chatur 0b013e31819301ab lall zeidan s67 s74 landry partiali oska barbat folbe takayesu arbelaez nadel mondoux okereke naim s1481803500012756 pololi pololi klotman arean lovett v15i0 sheri jyj chuah sel toh radha wingard garman reznik nearing nuechterlein zerzan libby bellon landry faiz s91 s92 ubel overtaxing nejmp2100179 abdelhamid elhawary gorgy akinla atiomo decastro sambuco ubel 0b013e318285d302','Abstract Study objective: Studies of mentorship in emergency medicine show that mentored residents are twice as likely to describe their career preparation as excellent as compared to nonmentored peers. There has been significant interest in the mentor–mentee relationship in medicine; however, there is minimal guidance and published literature specific to emergency medicine residents. Methods: In this narrative review, we described the emergency medicine mentor–mentee relationship, discussed alternatives to the traditional dyadic model, and highlighted current barriers to effective mentorship. We conducted a structured literature review to identify relevant published articles regarding the mentoring of emergency medicine residents. Additional studies from general mentoring literature were included based on relevancy. Results: We identified 39 studies in emergency medicine literature based on our search criteria. Additional studies from general medicine literature were included based on relevancy to this review. Based on the limited available literature, we recommend maximizing the resident mentoring relationship by developing formal mentoring programs, supporting the advancement of women and underrepresented minority mentors, and moving toward team mentoring, including peer, near-peer, and collaborative mentorship. The development of a mentoring network is a logical strategy for residents to work with a diverse group of individuals to maximize benefits in multiple areas. Conclusion: Alternative approaches to the traditional and hierarchal dyadic mentoring style (eg, team mentoring) are effective methods that residencies may promote to increase effective mentoring. Future efforts in mentoring emergency medicine residents emphasize these strategies, which are increasingly beneficial given the constraints and use of technology highlighted by the COVID-19 pandemic. Background Mentorship is a long-studied apprenticeship whose etymology dates back to Homer’s The Odyssey. As Odysseus prepared for war, he asked a trusted friend, Mentor, to act as a guardian and role model to his son Telemachus. 1 – 3 In the present day, a mentor provides guidance and takes a special interest in their mentee’s longitudinal development through dynamic and committed professional relationship. 4 – 6 True mentoring relationships are interactive; both the mentee and mentor obtain value from the collaboration. Mentorship in academic medicine has well-studied benefits, including increased career advancement, salary and rate of promotion, network connections, research productivity, and career satisfaction of mentors and mentees. 6 – 11 The mentor–mentee relationship varies widely; however, a genuine commitment by both parties is a key component to a successful relationship. 5 Practically, this basic tenet is complicated by resident training schedules, personal responsibilities with family and children, and – most recently – the disruptive effect of the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic. Studies of mentorship in emergency medicine show that mentored residents are twice as likely to describe their career preparation as excellent as compared to nonmentored peers, and 94.1% stated that mentorship was the key to success in residency. 8 , 12 Unfortunately, many residents do not have the opportunity to experience the benefits of mentoring because of limited available mentors and vague expectations of the mentoring relationship. 13 Historically, women and underrepresented minority (URM) emergency medicine residents face additional barriers to mentorship and have overall lower rates of mentorship. 5 , 6 , 12 , 14 – 18 Although there has been significant interest in the mentor–mentee relationship in medicine, there is minimal guidance and published literature specific to the mentorship of emergency medicine residents. 19 The last available review of the emergency medicine mentee relationship was completed in 2010 by Yeung et al, 12 at which time 13 potentially relevant studies were identified, and just one evaluated the mentorship of emergency medicine residents. 20 Historical reviews of mentorship and suggested best practices in emergency medicine were last published in Annals of Emergency Medicine over 2 decades ago. 2 , 21 In this review, we describe the emergency medicine mentor–mentee relationship and highlight current barriers to effective mentorship, including limited training on effective mentorship, formal mentoring programs, availability of women and URM mentors, and the ability of one mentor to address both the personal and professional needs of trainees based on the available literature in multiple specialties. 6 , 10 , 12 , 14 Although we focus on evidence specific to emergency medicine residents, we also evaluate relevant advice, strategies, and practices in general medicine as appropriate. We provide strategies and recommendations based on available literature to maximize this relationship, including the development of formal mentoring programs, supporting the advancement of women and URM mentors, and a movement toward team mentoring. 5 , 10 , 14 Methodology The authors performed a narrative review of the available literature on resident mentorship in emergency medicine. The literature search was performed using the PubMed and Google Scholar databases for papers published up to August 2022. Search terms included: “Emergency Medicine,” “resident,” “residency,” “mentor,” and “mentoring.” Articles were reviewed by 2 of the authors (J.S. and G.W.) for relevance, and any discrepancies were adjudicated by the third author (S.H.). Abstracts were reviewed for relevancy, and manuscripts that did not focus on mentoring resident physicians were excluded. Since 1998, 90 studies have met the PubMed search criteria in Figure 1 , of which 17 were specific to emergency medicine resident mentorship. Notably, only one study was a randomized controlled trial during this time period. A total of 39 papers were selected based on their relevance to mentorship for this manuscript, and 36 were included in this review. Figure 1. Open in a new tab The number of publications identified in PubMed on mentoring in emergency medicine. EM , emergency medicine. Mentoring 101: What Is a Mentor and How Are Successful Relationships Started? Although the role of the mentor has since been extensively described in the literature, a knowledge gap exists regarding the role of the mentor for residents, and it is often functionally more akin to an advisor or a coach ( Figure 2 ). Whereas an advisor may be an assigned faculty member in the learner’s preferred niche who provides practical advice and assessment of their strengths and weaknesses, the coach empowers the learner to drive their own goal-setting process and provides real-time feedback following direct observation. 4 , 22 These differ from a mentor, who takes a special interest in the longitudinal professional development of their mentee. 4 The traditional mentorship model is dyadic – in which a more senior member mentors a more junior colleague – and ideally benefits both the mentor and the mentee. 10 , 23 Figure 2. Open in a new tab Venn diagram describing general characteristics of mentors, advisors, and coaches. Included definitions of mentor, coach, and advisor can be found in accompanying references found in the Venn diagram. Studies recommend outlining expectations with clear duties and responsibilities of both the mentor and mentee at the start of the relationship. 10 , 24 Usually, this relationship is driven by engaged mentees with supportive mentors and appropriate institutional infrastructure. Common discussion topics include career guidance, research opportunities, and personal development. 14 Work-life balance is a primary focus of many mentoring relationships. 14 Evidence suggests that the mentor-mentee relationship may function as a coping mechanism and even improve wellness among residents. 8 Developing trust is a key component of the mentor–mentee relationship, allowing the mentee to act on the guidance provided by the mentor without fear of dismissal of their concerns. 2 By pairing a more senior mentor with a more junior mentee, the relationship may be restricted by hierarchical teacher-student interactions. 21 , 24 Some recommend modifying this relationship by encouraging socialization outside of work – if time and resources permit - and a focus on addressing general issues rather than an individual’s specific faults. 21 In turn, mentees should feel empowered to acknowledge their own shortcomings, accept criticism, modify their behavior, and cultivate a productive and sustainable relationship. 13 , 21 Training on Effective Mentorship “It [mentorship] is like a lot of things in academic medicine. Nobody teaches you how to do it, you are just expected to do it” - Straus et al 14 With the above reflections on the needs of the mentee, how does one become an effective mentor? A study by Shea et al 25 reported that less than 1 in 3 institutions could identify clear inclusion criteria for recruitment of mentors, whereas Feldman et al 26 found that less than 15% of mentors received formal training in mentorship. Some institutions have clearly defined expectations that senior faculty mentor one or more mentees and include annual training. 10 Training may include workshops, simulated mentorship, didactics, case-based learning, and “mentoring for mentors,” in which a new mentor shadows an established mentor. 14 , 27 They may include a mandatory initial training at the beginning of the academic year to clarify mentors’ roles and responsibilities. 27 Some academic centers have incorporated structured mentor training in hopes of increasing mentors’ self-confidence in their mentoring abilities. 6 , 10 , 28 A review of mentor training in medical education between 1990 to 2017 reported that mentor training improves mentor satisfaction and competency and boosts staff retention. 27 For example, the Colorado Mentoring Program provides 4 day-long training sessions throughout the academic year for both mentors and mentees. This intervention significantly improved mentor confidence in engaging in networking to assist their mentee and providing career guidance. Mentees reported that this intervention increased their connections to potential mentors, their ability to identify characteristics most important in a future mentor, and their prioritization of career objectives. 29 PROVIDING FORMAL MENTORSHIP The presence of formal mentoring relationships, in which a mentee is matched with a mentor, varies widely between programs. 19 , 30 In one survey of Canadian emergency medicine residents, nearly two-thirds had at least one mentor in residency and those programs with a formal mentorship program were significantly more likely to receive mentorship as compared to those without. 8 The presence of formal mentorship in a survey of anesthesiology programs was significantly correlated with higher rates of identified mentors (82% vs 17%) and residents were more likely to interact with their mentor (70% vs 46%). 5 These mentor–mentee relationships may be organic - through self-selection - or arranged by the institution with the goal of providing at least one mentor throughout training. 9 Assigned mentorship may reduce barriers to finding a mentor, though it yields mixed results in the available literature. Nearly half of the surveyed anesthesiology residents were either satisfied or very satisfied with their assigned mentor, whereas a survey of mentees in academic medicine suggests that assigned mentor relationships may seem “forced” and that the most productive relationships were spontaneous. 25 A study by Keyser et al 10 recommends that institutions provide future mentees with a list of senior faculty who are interested in mentoring, including their areas of expertise and networking opportunities, to facilitate the development of an effective mentor–mentee relationship. If an institution lacks local mentorship, it may consider identifying external mentors and facilitating in-person or virtual structured networking opportunities. 14 , 31 Increasing Availability of Women Mentors Women account for roughly one-half of all medical school graduates but only one-fourth of all emergency medicine residents. 17 Women emergency medicine residents face additional barriers to obtaining career mentors, resulting in lower rates of mentorship and reduced promotion to senior academic and leadership positions. 5 , 6 , 12 , 14 – 18 Nearly 1 in 4 women junior faculty at the University of California identified “lack of mentorship as one of the most negative experiences in their career” and a major obstacle to career advancement. 6 , 12 Studies suggest that women mentees prefer women mentors and note that a male mentor may not be able to provide guidance on issues of parental leave and its effect on career trajectory. 14 Sex concordance is associated with more highly rated mentoring relationships; however, this is often difficult to achieve because of the reduced numbers of available women mentors in the field. 5 , 15 , 18 Efforts should be made to match women mentees with women mentors, if possible, and improved access to potential women mentors may be achieved through both recruitment and networking efforts ( Table 1 ). Limited available data advocates for recruiting women emergency medicine faculty, creating formal mentorship and faculty development programs, and networking in local or national women professional development groups. 15 , 18 In studies by the University of California, San Diego (UCSD) and Creighton University, the presence of formal mentorship and faculty development programs increased recruitment of women and minority faculty in family medicine from 58% to 80% at UCSD and 20% to 58% at Creighton University. 32 Professional development group involvement has been shown to improve rates of mentorship and expansion of peer support networks. 15 Table 1. Women and underrepresented minority medical group networking resources. Society Women URM American Academy of Emergency Medicine (AAEM) Women in Emergency Medicine Section (WiEMs) Academy for Diversity and Inclusion in Emergency Medicine Society for Academic Emergency Medicine (SAEM) Academy of Women in Academic Emergency Medicine (AWAEM) Justice, Equity, Diversity, and Inclusion (JEDI-AAEM) Section American Medical Association (AMA) Women Physicians Section (WPS) Minority Affairs Section (MAS) American Association of Medical Colleges Group on Women in Medicine and Science (GWIMS) Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Initiatives American Medical Women’s Association (AMWA) AMWA Females Working in Emergency Medicine (FemInEM) FemInEM NA Women in Medicine (WIM) WIM NA Association of American Indian Physicians (AAIP) NA AAIP National Council of Asian Pacific Islander Physicians (NCAPIP) NA NCAPIP National Hispanic Medical Association (NHMA) NA NHMA Open in a new tab Increasing Availability of URM Mentors URM account for almost one-third of the US population but less than 10% of practicing emergency physicians. 16 This lack of racial and ethnic diversity in the emergency medicine workforce has been cited as a major contributor to inequality in health care for minority patients and limits available mentorship opportunities for URM emergency physicians. 16 , 32 URM emergency medicine residents face significant barriers to obtaining effective career mentorship with deleterious effects on promotion rates to senior academic and leadership roles. 5 , 6 , 12 , 14 – 18 Previous literature notes that URM physicians may prefer racial concordance in mentorship. 5 , 31 This is complicated by the limited availability of URM mentors and may unfairly place the burden on a few URM mentors to mentor several mentees. The minority tax describes the burden of overtime, unpaid work by URM professionals to drive diversity initiatives, thus limiting their available time to focus on more traditionally rewarded activities for professional advancement. 33 Although beyond the scope of this manuscript, the minority tax is a large and critically important topic. Williamson et al 33 recommend decreasing URM participation in committees to decrease time demands, engaging URM faculty in URM initiatives later in their career to allow for more time to focus on personal career advancement, encouraging White faculty to participate in diversity initiatives, and ensuring URM committee involvement is weighed in promotion considerations. They also recommend protected time for URM faculty for these efforts through grants and administrative support. Several studies suggest increasing opportunities for URM mentorship through the recruitment of URM faculty, the creation of URM faculty development programs, and the expansion of mentorship networks outside of local institutions, both virtually and in person ( Table 1 ). 15 , 16 , 18 , 31 , 32 The aforementioned faculty development program at UCSD emphasized mentorship and career advancement strategies for URM faculty, yielding a 92% promotion rate for URM Family Medicine faculty who completed the course. 32 A similar program at Morehouse University yielded increased retention of URM faculty in family medicine from 33% to 81%. 32 Virtual mentorship has increased significantly in the wake of COVID-19 and has proven to be successful in increasing available mentorship relationships for URM junior faculty, residents, and medical students. 31 Alternative Approaches to Mentoring: Team Mentorship, Peer and Near-Peer Mentoring, and Mentor Networks “It is a myth that the mentor should be a single person with all the answers. In fact, a mentee may need several mentors, each pertaining to a different career aspect” - Yeung et al 12 As the roles and responsibilities of both the mentor and mentee expand, it may become increasingly difficult for a single mentor to address each of the needs of their resident-mentee. 9 Sixty-three percent of emergency medicine residents in one study reported having more than one mentor during residency. 8 Others have noted that it may be difficult to find one single mentor with the necessary experience, skills, interest, and personality to facilitate a productive mentoring relationship. 10 Osborn et al 2 recommend that one may have separate mentors in distinct personal or professional areas of development and that mentorship may occur remotely, through telephone, email, or national meetings. Others note the utility of having a local institutional academic mentor (who provides guidance on career trajectory, navigating local politics, and work-life balance) and a local or remote research mentor. 14 Additionally, this use of “team mentorship” may allow less experienced mentors to share the responsibility of resident mentorship, hone their mentorship skills, and relieve mentors utilizing the traditional dyadic model from time constraints. 34 Others suggest the development of peer or near-peer collaborative group mentorship. 23 , 24 , 35 These alternatives to traditional mentorship expand mentor networks by utilizing academic peers of the same training level or near-peers – in which one peer is one or more years ahead in training - to develop structured development plans with guidance from a facilitator. 23 , 24 , 35 Pololi et al 23 , 24 found that - as compared to traditional dyadic mentorship - peer or collaborative group mentoring among junior academic medicine faculty generated a nurturing and productive environment and yielded higher rates of program attendance and personal empowerment. This is maybe particularly beneficial at institutions that lack enough senior faculty to properly serve as mentors to residents. 23 , 24 , 35 Finally, the benefits of “mentoring networks” have been described among junior research faculty as an alternative to the traditional and hierarchal dyadic mentoring approach. 36 Similar to team mentoring, this concept is based on the fact that a single mentor can rarely (if ever) provide all components a mentee requires for success. Rather, a diverse group of mentors can provide expertise to the trainee in areas of their respective strengths. 36 Although the onus of developing a mentor network is on the trainee, this may be particularly beneficial to emergency medicine residents who lack home institution faculty expertise in certain areas. In light of the COVID-19 pandemic’s need for social distancing, mentoring networks through virtual mentorship have become an asset by removing geographic boundaries and allowing the expansion of mentorship networks for the mentee. 14 , 31 , 34 DISCUSSION The mentor–mentee relationship is a crucial component of medical training and may be improved through the development of formal mentoring programs, training for mentors, support for the advancement of women and URM mentors, and nondyadic mentoring models. There is no singular approach to developing a successful mentoring relationship, and institutions often have their own strategy and limitations to mentorship. A review of available literature suggests that there is often a lack of available mentors, particularly for women and URM. Importantly, residents must realize that the traditional and hierarchal dyadic model for mentorship may not be the ideal mentoring approach. 2 , 5 , 9 , 10 , 12 , 14 , 15 , 17 , 21 , 22 , 24 , 33 , 34 We encourage the reader to consider alternative approaches to the dyadic mentoring relationship in the future, such as team, peer, or near-peer collaborative mentorship and mentoring networks, to expand mentoring opportunities to mentees. As shown by Pololi et al, 23 , 24 this model may be useful in the future for effectively training young mentors as well. Further, since the last published reviews of mentorship in Annals of Emergency Medicine in 2001 by Stack et al, 21 the availability of virtual mentorship provides avenues for significantly expanded mentoring networks. 2 , 14 , 34 In addition to the strategies and recommendations provided above, we emphasize that mentee engagement - particularly at the beginning of a mentoring relationship - is necessary to develop a meaningful experience for both mentee and mentor. 12 It has been the authors’ observation that the best organic mentoring relationships may occur after a period of adjustment to the resident role. A potential strategy may entail deferring the selection of possible mentors until the resident has developed professional relationships with various attendings and reflected on their personal and professional goals. Table 2 provides various strategies to maximize mentoring, though we believe numerous challenges exist which serve as potential targets to improve mentoring of emergency medicine residents. To begin with, it is the author’s experience that many residents confuse a residency-assigned advisor or coach with that mentor. A potential solution is to emphasize the differences between advisor, coach, and mentor early in residency training. This may then allow residents to identify better individuals that can function as mentors throughout their training (and beyond). Next, it is not uncommon that institutions lack qualified mentors for residents, particularly female and URM trainees. Various strategies exist to mitigate this, including increased institutional and departmental support (potentially including protected time) and an emphasis on mentorship training early during faculty career development. Residencies may train senior residents to function as peer or near-peer mentors, thereby developing the next generation of emergency medicine mentors in the process. Table 2. Resident mentoring concerns and potential solutions. Concern Potential Solution Mentee factors Unknown role of mentor, mentee Clarify the role of a mentor vs advisor vs coach early in residency Outline expectations, duties, and responsibilities of mentor and mentee early on Finding a mentor Create formal residency mentorship programs Provide future mentees with a list of preidentified potential mentors to allow for the selection of an ideal mentor Consider delaying the selection of a mentor after adjustment to the resident role Limited available mentors Increase training of mentors, recruitment of women, and URM mentors Move toward team, peer, or near-peer mentorship and mentoring networks Aid in identifying mentors at outside institutions and groups, including virtually Mentor factors Unknown role of mentor, mentee Clarify institutional expectations of the mentor Develop peer, near-peer mentoring of junior faculty and senior residents Limited training or experience in mentoring Provide training workshops, simulated mentorship, or didactics at the beginning of or throughout the academic year Ability of one mentor to meet the needs of the mentee Move toward team, peer, or near-peer mentorship and mentoring networks Organizational factors Availability of women mentors Recruit and focus on the professional development of women junior faculty Provide support for participation in national and virtual groups and conferences Availability of URM mentors Recruit and focus on the professional development of URM junior faculty Provide support for participation in national and virtual groups and conferences Encourage non-URM faculty to participate in diversity initiatives and committees Weigh URM committee involvement in promotion considerations Provide protected time to URM faculty for their efforts in URM initiatives through grants and administrative support Limited protected time for mentorship Provide institutionally backed protected time for mentorship Consider including a mentorship role in the promotion and tenure decisions Open in a new tab In conclusion, future efforts should emphasize nondyadic mentoring models, although we note there is a lack of quantitative and evidence-based literature specific to emergency medicine resident mentorship. In general, however, mentorship is well studied, and strategies from other specialties, medical fields, and the broader academic community may be utilized to develop longitudinal, mutually enriching, and personally rewarding relationships. Funding and support: By Annals policy, all authors are required to disclose any and all commercial, financial, and other relationships in any way related to the subject of this article as per ICMJE conflict of interest guidelines (see www.icmje.org ). The authors have stated that no such relationships exist. Footnotes Supervising editors: Jake Toy, DO; Jason D. Heiner, MD. Specific detailed information about possible conflict of interest for individual editors is available at https://www.annemergmed.com/editors . Contributor Information Julia Sobel, Department of Emergency Medicine, UC San Diego Health, San Diego. Stephen R. Hayden, Department of Emergency Medicine, UC San Diego Health, San Diego; Department of Emergency Medicine, UHS SoCal MEC – Temecula Valley Hospital, Temecula. Gabriel Wardi, Department of Emergency Medicine, UC San Diego Health, San Diego; Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care, and Sleep Medicine, UC San Diego Health, La Jolla. REFERENCES 1. Garmel GM. Mentoring medical students in academic emergency medicine. Acad Emerg Med. 2004;11:1351–1357. 10.1197/j.aem.2004.06.013 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Osborn TM, Waeckerle JF, Perina D, Keyes LE. Mentorship: Through the Looking Glass Into Our Future. Ann Emerg Med. 1999;34:285–289. 10.1016/S0196-0644(99)70245-3 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Larson AS, Haines SJ, Pilcher WH, Piva TE, Grande AW. From the historical examples of Drs. Osler, Cushing, and Van Wagenen: lessons on the importance of mentorship in contemporary neurosurgery. World Neurosurg. 2020;140:251–257. 10.1016/j.wneu.2020.05.100 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lomis KD, Santen SA, Dekhtyar M, et al. The Accelerating Change in Medical Education Consortium: Key Drivers of Transformative Change. Acad Med. 2021;96:979–988. 10.1097/ACM.0000000000003897 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Zakus P, Gelb AW, Flexman AM. A survey of mentorship among Canadian anesthesiology residents. Can J Anaesth. 2015;62:972–978. 10.1007/s12630-015-0418-8 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Sambunjak D, Straus SE, Marušić A. Mentoring in academic medicine: a systematic review. JAMA. 2006;296:1103–1115. 10.1001/jama.296.9.1103 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Coates WC. Being a mentor: what’s in it for me?: BEING. Acad Emerg Med. 2012;19:92–97. 10.1111/j.1553-2712.2011.01258.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Fernando SM, Cheung WJ, Choi SB, Thurgur L, Frank JR. Faculty mentorship during residency and professional development among practising emergency physicians. CJEM. 2018;20:944–951. 10.1